WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:06.120
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:11.120
Welcome to this month's
monthly inference colloquium

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:16.250
as part of our series
on trying to understand

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:19.800
the nature of correlation and causation.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.400
And it gives me great
pleasure to introduce

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.103
our wonderful speaker for the talk today,

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:30.970
Dr. Eric Winsberg.

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:34.900
But before I give you a brief introduction

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:38.670
to his intellectual
trajectory and his work,

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:42.570
I would like to say
thanks to our benefactors,

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.530
Mr. and Mrs. Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:48.780
who are unfortunately
not joining us today,

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.380
but their support of the Franke Program

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:55.400
and many other activities at Yale

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.270
is gratefully acknowledged.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.430
So I also want to remind all of you

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:02.550
that we are recording this event,

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:04.890
and therefore all participants must

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:06.890
therefore mute their videos.

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:11.890
And as per usual and customary
at the end of Eric's talk,

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:16.050
we will have your questions,

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:17.970
which you can feel free to type up

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:20.930
in the chat as we go along,

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:24.920
and we'll have a Q and A
session at the very end.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.300
So I also wanted to remind everyone

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.760
that in addition to today's lecture

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:33.610
in this colloquium series,

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:36.550
we have sort of the discussion session

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:37.630
that happens tomorrow,

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:42.060
at the same time, at 3:00 PM tomorrow,

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:45.890
we will have a discussion of Eric Winsberg

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:48.310
and the conversant this time is going

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:51.504
to be Dr. Stephanie Harvard,

00:01:51.504 --> 00:01:56.220
who is a postdoctoral research fellow

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.660
at the University of British Columbia,

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:03.430
and in the faculty of
Pharmaceutical Sciences.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:07.313
So first moving on to today's talk,

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:12.030
I just wanted to give you a brief sense

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:17.030
of Eric's intellectual path
and his research interests.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.630
I first got to know of Eric

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:24.640
when I read his work on simulations,

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:29.640
really radical rethinking of
how to understand simulations

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:34.090
and modeling in the context of sciences,

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.470
and in range of sciences.

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:39.490
So Eric got his PhD from Indiana,

00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:43.430
and following a postdoctoral
fellowship in History

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:46.090
and Philosophy of Science at Northwestern,

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:48.450
he joined the Philosophy Department

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.667
as a faculty member at USF,

00:02:52.474 --> 00:02:57.474
and he went there in 2001
and has remained there since.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:01.420
His principle interests are
in the Philosophy of Science

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:03.920
in particular, the
Philosophy of Climate Science

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:06.300
and the Philosophy of Physics.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.960
He's particularly interested in the role

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:12.530
of computer simulations
in physical sciences,

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:15.220
and analog simulation in cosmology,

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:17.880
and in the foundations
of statistical physics

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:20.230
and the direction of time.

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:23.050
Many of you would have heard him,

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:27.220
he was our conversant with Carlo Rovelli,

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:29.753
which was sort of the
opening talk of our series.

00:03:32.526 --> 00:03:37.526
He also writes on truth and
on scientific authorship,

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:40.940
it's a really wide
ranging set of interests.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:43.820
He's the author of several
articles on all of these topics

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:45.970
that are appeared in all the top journals,

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:48.070
like Philosophy of Science,
Journal of Philosophy,

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:50.710
the British Journal for
the Philosophy of Science,

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:51.610
Studies in History,

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:54.930
and Philosophy of Modern
Physics and Synthese.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:58.280
He has held numerous visiting fellowships

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:03.440
and has a lot of honors,

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.060
was a fellow at the
Institute for Advanced Study

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:08.430
at the University of Durham in the UK,

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:10.250
at the University of California, Berkeley,

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:11.890
at MCMP in Munich,

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:15.710
and the University of
Lunenburg in Germany.

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:20.710
So he has two wonderful
books, which are out already.

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:24.930
The first one is "Science in
the Age of Computer Simulation"

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:27.320
from the University of Chicago Press,

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:31.400
and "Philosophy and Climate Science",

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.890
which was published by
Cambridge University Press.

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:35.973
And he has a forthcoming book,

00:04:35.973 --> 00:04:38.422
Cambridge Elements in
Philosophy of Science

00:04:38.422 --> 00:04:39.770
on Modeled in Science.

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:43.680
Over the last year during the pandemic,

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:47.080
Eric has focused on the
use of models and methods

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:50.930
of causal inference in
the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:53.730
extremely important work
as many of you know,

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:55.600
that during the pandemic,

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:59.470
we really saw the provisional
nature of science,

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:03.780
and how we can draw limited inferences.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:06.680
As we get to know more and
more about the disease,

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:09.530
we saw all of that play out
right in front of our eyes.

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:12.860
We all had front row seats
to how this was happening,

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:15.568
so really look forward to hearing Eric

00:05:15.568 --> 00:05:16.560
some of your thoughts on this,

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:20.963
and hopefully you will cover
some of this material today.

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:25.653
So when he's not tapping
away on his laptop working,

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.130
you can often find` them on the water,

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:30.780
on the standup paddle board,

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:34.310
or that is the joy of people
who live in lovely climates.

00:05:34.310 --> 00:05:35.270
What can I say?

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:37.270
Very envious Eric.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:39.750
So the title of his talk today

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:43.580
is causal inference in
complex non-linear systems,

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:45.170
a cautionary tale.

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:46.750
So thank you so much, Eric,

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:48.970
for agreeing to speak in this series

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:53.800
and we are absolutely delighted
and take it away, please.

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:56.140
- Thank you Priya, I wanna
see if I can get this

00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:57.200
Okay, great.

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:58.780
Just to give people a
little bit background,

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:01.100
Priya has already told
you that I've worked a lot

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:03.340
on Philosophy of Climate Science,

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:06.600
I finished my book up on
"Philosophy of Climate Science"

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:08.203
in about 2017.

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:11.950
And I was going out to California,

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:13.830
a year and a half ago.

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:16.521
And I wanted to start
thinking a little bit more

00:06:16.521 --> 00:06:19.360
about how modeling can inform policy.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:21.900
'Cause I've really been
focusing mostly just on them,

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:23.946
not modeling itself.

00:06:23.946 --> 00:06:26.546
And I sort of started
to make that transition

00:06:26.546 --> 00:06:27.710
to thinking more about having a more sort

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:30.950
of policy oriented focus to my research,

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:33.930
and that was in January of 2020.

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:36.040
And then we all know what happened

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:38.250
in the next couple of months,

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:40.270
which made it pretty obvious to me

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:44.930
that I ought to be focusing on this,

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:48.920
very new area in which
models were informing policy,

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:53.920
in a pretty quick and
impactful sort of way.

00:06:54.290 --> 00:06:58.360
And so one of the things I hope
we can sort of get around to

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:00.740
in the session is talking about

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:05.050
what kinds of ways
modeling can inform policy,

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:06.470
and what constraints there ought to be

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:08.953
on models that inform
policy, that kind of stuff.

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:13.950
But because of the sort of
theme of the conference,

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:16.837
I wanted to just focus a lot on,

00:07:16.837 --> 00:07:20.290
the theme of this series
being the relationship

00:07:20.290 --> 00:07:21.670
between correlation and causation.

00:07:21.670 --> 00:07:25.010
I wanted to focus on some of
the stuff I've been watching

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:27.150
about how we've made causal inferences

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:28.710
over the course of the pandemic.

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:30.790
And then I'm hoping like tomorrow

00:07:30.790 --> 00:07:32.880
as Stephanie joins the conversation,

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:34.850
we can kind of broaden
the conversation back

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:38.690
to the general topic of
how models inform policy.

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:41.541
Okay, so let's see, how's this, okay.

00:07:41.541 --> 00:07:43.823
So a lot of this kind of started early,

00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:49.220
my interest in this kind of
started early in the pandemic,

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:51.931
and I had to go back
to the way back machine

00:07:51.931 --> 00:07:53.037
on the internet to find this,

00:07:53.037 --> 00:07:54.740
'cause unfortunately I didn't
screenshot at the time,

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:59.740
but this was the Financial
Times (indistinct) March 29th,

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:04.760
I think it is 2020, showing
us the course of the pandemic

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:07.420
in a variety of countries.

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:10.450
And what things I found
fascinating about this chart

00:08:10.450 --> 00:08:15.450
was all these little blurbs
that they inserted here,

00:08:16.210 --> 00:08:18.930
sort of explaining the
course of the pandemic

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:20.712
in all these countries.

00:08:20.712 --> 00:08:21.990
So Italy had the highest death rate

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:24.350
because of a large elderly population.

00:08:24.350 --> 00:08:26.040
South Korea had a low death rate

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:29.400
because of early large-scale
testing and tracing,

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:32.870
Spain locked down after 200 days,

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:35.340
but France locked down after 175 days,

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:39.170
and that's why Spain is a
little bit ahead of France.

00:08:39.170 --> 00:08:41.030
Japan, I had to write this one in myself

00:08:41.030 --> 00:08:43.180
'cause I definitely
remember this being on here,

00:08:43.180 --> 00:08:45.000
but I didn't find the
version that said it,

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:48.160
Japan had a culture of masks and Bali,

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:51.763
and that was responsible for
where they are on the chart.

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:56.260
And a lot of what this
immediately reminded of was

00:08:57.617 --> 00:09:01.170
an episode in the history
of climate science,

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:04.850
which kind of lasted from
about 1998 till 2012,

00:09:04.850 --> 00:09:08.610
and it was known as the Warming Hiatus.

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:11.960
And there was a lot of
expelled over this hiatus

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:16.010
that was thought to have been
happening between 1998, 2012.

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:18.740
And you can kind of see it here, right?

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:22.320
So the green line is the concentration

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:24.460
of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere,

00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:28.570
which all climate
scientists agreed results

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:30.600
in warming of the planet,

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:32.620
and yet this red line here,

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:36.850
which was tracing out the global
average surface temperature

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:41.310
of the planet seemed to
hit kind of a slow patch

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:43.200
in about 1998.

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:47.991
As you can see in the chart
between 1998 and 2012,

00:09:47.991 --> 00:09:51.270
there wasn't a lot of net
warming of the planet.

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:54.760
And this caused a lot of consternation

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:56.570
among a variety of parties.

00:09:56.570 --> 00:09:59.120
And people immediately wanted to know why,

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:00.853
why is there this hiatus?

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:03.620
And of course,

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:08.620
people who were opponents
of climate mitigation,

00:10:08.690 --> 00:10:12.340
who were doubters of the
existence of climate change,

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:14.500
that clearly should be
a major problem, right?

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:17.890
So this was the Telegraph in 2006,

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:20.170
there is a problem with global warming,

00:10:20.170 --> 00:10:22.623
and that it stopped in 1998.

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:27.920
And immediately I think
climate scientists felt

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:29.840
the need to explain this.

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:33.480
Why, why did the warming stop in 1998?

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:35.790
And there were, I think, sort
of two causal questions here,

00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:37.460
two explanations.

00:10:37.460 --> 00:10:41.820
One was why the slow down, right?

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:44.520
So just the brute fact that the warming

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:47.543
of the planet seemed to have paused maybe.

00:10:48.495 --> 00:10:50.290
So just the brute fact itself.

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:52.260
And then there was this what was perceived

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:54.150
to be this modeling mismatch, right?

00:10:54.150 --> 00:10:57.410
So if you look at this picture,

00:10:57.410 --> 00:11:00.640
what you see the kind of
grayish black line here,

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:03.700
that's kind of heading inexorably upward,

00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:07.690
that was the predictions
of the so-called the CMIP,

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:10.010
the climate model inter
comparison project,

00:11:10.010 --> 00:11:12.210
which kind of averages together,

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:14.420
the forecast of a variety
of climate models.

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:16.500
And then all these little colored lines,

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:21.500
which are the actual datasets
of various meteorological

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:25.300
and climate agencies around the world,

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:28.756
like the Hadley center in
the UK National Atmosphere,

00:11:28.756 --> 00:11:31.775
(indistinct) in the states.

00:11:31.775 --> 00:11:36.775
So, there was this brute fact
warming seems to have paused

00:11:37.730 --> 00:11:39.570
and then this modeling mismatch.

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:41.043
And the question was why,

00:11:42.404 --> 00:11:45.930
and there were sort of a
variety of explanations.

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:50.440
This is from a paper in 2015 by Thorne,

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:53.050
and others kind of going through a variety

00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:57.920
of plausible explanations
of why the pause happened.

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:00.570
Maybe it was variations in El Nino,

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:02.420
maybe it was some volcanic eruptions,

00:12:02.420 --> 00:12:05.220
maybe it was quiet solar cycle,

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:08.290
maybe it was changes in
stratospheric water vapor.

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:11.040
So, climate scientists, I
think, felt the pressure

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:13.810
to explain this pause came up

00:12:13.810 --> 00:12:15.770
with a variety of explanations.

00:12:15.770 --> 00:12:19.757
And then of course, the other side said,

00:12:19.757 --> 00:12:22.970
"Oh, they're just looking at
all these excuses," right?

00:12:22.970 --> 00:12:27.970
So this is this kind of
notorious climate skeptic blog,

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:29.090
Watts Up With That?

00:12:30.470 --> 00:12:33.830
And here you get from 2014 a list

00:12:33.830 --> 00:12:36.793
of excuses for the pause.

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:41.330
Of course in the end,

00:12:41.330 --> 00:12:43.730
I need to start to drag my
windows, I can see this.

00:12:43.730 --> 00:12:47.090
In the end, as we look at
this, running all the way from,

00:12:47.090 --> 00:12:50.280
let's say about 1900 up to the present,

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:52.160
you can still see this little pause here

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:56.397
between about 1998 and about 2012,

00:12:56.397 --> 00:12:59.020
but in the great scheme of things,

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:02.500
it's pretty clear that nothing
particularly interesting

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:05.000
or important was happening here,

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:07.160
that was just maybe a little bit

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:10.670
of internal variability
swamping the overall trends,

00:13:10.670 --> 00:13:15.600
so the clear trend here from
1900 to about 2020 is warming.

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:18.620
And you get in this spot 14 year period,

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:20.170
for whatever reason,

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:22.020
you get some internal
variability in the climate,

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:24.353
that is swamping the trends.

00:13:26.390 --> 00:13:28.860
And then, also I think
one of the other things

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:31.020
that people sort of overlooked in,

00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:33.120
if we go back and we look at this picture,

00:13:34.122 --> 00:13:38.300
why is there this sort
of pause in the data,

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:41.140
like this very smooth
increase in the predictions,

00:13:41.140 --> 00:13:44.020
the models, why don't
the models also predict

00:13:44.020 --> 00:13:46.760
this kind of internal
variability you might ask

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:49.520
and he answered there, I
think it's also rather simple.

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:54.440
The sea map it's a climate
model inter comparison project.

00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:59.440
So it's an amalgam,
average is over a variety

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:00.930
of different climate models,

00:14:00.930 --> 00:14:03.967
and that's just apt to smooth out,

00:14:03.967 --> 00:14:05.030
that just have to smooth it out.

00:14:05.030 --> 00:14:07.440
Each model probably had a similar amount

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:10.640
of internal variability
as the actual day-to-day,

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:13.110
but when you average them together,

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:15.870
you got something to look
quite a bit smoother.

00:14:15.870 --> 00:14:17.390
Okay.

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:20.223
So there was a rather simple
answer to the question,

00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:25.080
why was there a warming hiatus?

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:26.660
And the real answer was, well,

00:14:26.660 --> 00:14:28.720
it's a complex non-linear system,

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:31.400
and complex non-linear
systems just do weird things.

00:14:31.400 --> 00:14:36.400
They behave in kind of sporadic
and jumpy and clumpy ways.

00:14:38.620 --> 00:14:40.190
And there's a danger, right?

00:14:40.190 --> 00:14:42.800
I think when you see the
behavior of non-linear systems

00:14:42.800 --> 00:14:45.430
and that's gonna be one
of my main themes today,

00:14:45.430 --> 00:14:48.374
there's a danger when you
see the erratic behavior

00:14:48.374 --> 00:14:49.207
of a non-linear system to think

00:14:49.207 --> 00:14:51.260
there must be an explanation
for these things.

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:54.420
I think we saw that happen quite a bit

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:58.500
in that period of climate history

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:00.253
when there was this analysis.

00:15:01.090 --> 00:15:03.440
And so when I started
seeing these pictures

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:07.410
in Financial Times with
all of these explanations

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:10.423
of each one of these graphs,

00:15:12.890 --> 00:15:15.690
it sort of set my alarm bells off,

00:15:15.690 --> 00:15:18.623
maybe something similar is going on here.

00:15:19.520 --> 00:15:23.040
And another sort of, I
think thing that we saw

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:24.840
in the climate hiatus cases,

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:27.917
that data rise is uncertain, right.

00:15:27.917 --> 00:15:28.750
Data rise is uncertain.

00:15:28.750 --> 00:15:32.684
And one of the things that drives all of

00:15:32.684 --> 00:15:35.563
the mystery in this chart,

00:15:37.160 --> 00:15:38.990
is that supposedly, sorry, supposedly,

00:15:38.990 --> 00:15:42.780
all of these graphs supposedly,

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:47.780
are synchronized beginning
when the number of deaths

00:15:48.370 --> 00:15:49.793
in that country reached 10.

00:15:51.870 --> 00:15:54.642
But we now know, I think in retrospect

00:15:54.642 --> 00:15:56.660
that this was all a fantasy, right?

00:15:56.660 --> 00:15:58.870
We had no idea when
each of these countries

00:15:58.870 --> 00:16:00.990
reached their 10th death.

00:16:00.990 --> 00:16:04.310
There was a nice, recent,
really careful recent paper

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:07.860
in the journal of infectious diseases,

00:16:07.860 --> 00:16:11.160
I think it was that I get so many people

00:16:11.160 --> 00:16:12.430
have been doing now for months,

00:16:12.430 --> 00:16:14.020
but did it very carefully,

00:16:14.020 --> 00:16:19.020
and showed that by sort of
middle of January, 2020,

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:24.040
there were already cases
of COVID-19 infection

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:29.350
in six or seven US States.

00:16:29.430 --> 00:16:32.840
So they just went and
looked at blood donors

00:16:32.840 --> 00:16:33.880
from that period.

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:35.420
So, we really don't know.

00:16:35.420 --> 00:16:37.962
We really don't know when
any of these countries

00:16:37.962 --> 00:16:39.401
had their tenth death.

00:16:39.401 --> 00:16:40.730
And so all of this lining
off and all of this,

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:42.930
like that was all relatively,

00:16:42.930 --> 00:16:47.278
just an artifact of the way that

00:16:47.278 --> 00:16:48.520
the data was being collected at the time.

00:16:48.520 --> 00:16:49.930
We really don't know how to line up

00:16:49.930 --> 00:16:52.258
these different countries with respect

00:16:52.258 --> 00:16:54.323
to when their epidemic started.

00:16:55.640 --> 00:16:59.180
And sort of over the
course of the pandemic,

00:16:59.180 --> 00:17:04.180
we've been subjected to a
lot of causal inferences.

00:17:05.480 --> 00:17:08.810
And I think one of the things that happens

00:17:08.810 --> 00:17:11.140
when you have a lot of noisy data,

00:17:11.140 --> 00:17:14.713
with a lot of clumpy,
pretty jerky behavior in it,

00:17:15.650 --> 00:17:18.568
people will often find
what they wanna find

00:17:18.568 --> 00:17:20.440
just as in the climate case,

00:17:20.440 --> 00:17:23.520
people who didn't
believe in climate change

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:24.930
took that as an opportunity to say

00:17:24.930 --> 00:17:26.360
that they were finding this.

00:17:26.360 --> 00:17:29.260
So I think in the pandemic,
people have been able

00:17:29.260 --> 00:17:30.840
to find what they wanna find.

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:35.840
So here's an article that ran
into a bunch of news outlets,

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:38.960
I think the associated press started this,

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:40.960
it's from right after the election,

00:17:40.960 --> 00:17:43.520
obviously November 6th 2020.

00:17:43.520 --> 00:17:45.540
And it tells us that US counties

00:17:45.540 --> 00:17:47.720
with the highest virus surges

00:17:47.720 --> 00:17:49.663
overwhelmingly voted for Trump.

00:17:51.400 --> 00:17:54.707
And when this came out,
my first thought was okay,

00:17:54.707 --> 00:17:56.310
well, maybe that's just some kind of

00:17:57.440 --> 00:18:00.910
again just some kind of
spurious correlation here,

00:18:00.910 --> 00:18:02.650
maybe there's something to this,

00:18:02.650 --> 00:18:04.360
but then of course, once you've dug

00:18:04.360 --> 00:18:05.530
into the data a little bit,

00:18:05.530 --> 00:18:09.898
what you realized was that
it's true that the US counties

00:18:09.898 --> 00:18:10.731
with the highest buyer surges

00:18:10.731 --> 00:18:12.230
overwhelmingly voted for Trump,

00:18:12.230 --> 00:18:13.560
but it's also true that the counties

00:18:13.560 --> 00:18:17.530
with the lowest voter surges
overwhelmingly voted for Trump.

00:18:17.530 --> 00:18:20.150
And there's a simple explanation for this.

00:18:20.150 --> 00:18:23.830
And that's that the counties
that voted for Trump

00:18:23.830 --> 00:18:28.620
were on the whole smaller counties

00:18:28.620 --> 00:18:30.570
than the counties that voted for Biden.

00:18:32.185 --> 00:18:35.860
Obviously we all know that rural
America tended to vote more

00:18:35.860 --> 00:18:38.200
for Trump and urban
America voted for Biden,

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:40.100
rural counties are smaller,

00:18:40.100 --> 00:18:42.350
small samples have more variants.

00:18:42.350 --> 00:18:47.350
And so the Trump counties
tended to have either

00:18:47.740 --> 00:18:49.640
very large numbers of Corona virus,

00:18:49.640 --> 00:18:51.170
or very low numbers of Corona virus.

00:18:51.170 --> 00:18:54.957
They just had the noisier samples and all.

00:18:55.922 --> 00:18:57.613
And that was primarily
what was going on here.

00:18:59.497 --> 00:19:02.970
And then we also, soon
we were looking at people

00:19:02.970 --> 00:19:06.440
were trying to find
evidence of that various,

00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:09.803
public health measures of
various kinds were effective.

00:19:10.929 --> 00:19:13.871
If people remember this right,

00:19:13.871 --> 00:19:18.871
but we very quickly went
from a pretty broad consensus

00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:23.040
in say, February and March of 2020

00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:24.948
among public health experts,

00:19:24.948 --> 00:19:26.983
that masks were not really something

00:19:26.983 --> 00:19:29.483
that the public ought
to be concerned about.

00:19:30.430 --> 00:19:34.310
And then quickly that
scientific consensus changed,

00:19:34.310 --> 00:19:37.343
and so there was a lot of
work trying to show this,

00:19:38.720 --> 00:19:43.720
and a lot of it really kind of
was a pretty quick and dirty.

00:19:45.810 --> 00:19:49.353
So this was a study that came out in,

00:19:51.820 --> 00:19:54.300
it was published in October of 2020,

00:19:55.670 --> 00:19:57.470
got reported in a lot of outlets.

00:19:57.470 --> 00:20:00.603
This is a CNN article that's
referring back to this study.

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:04.240
And what was the study doing?

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:09.240
The study was merely going
back to kind of late April,

00:20:09.610 --> 00:20:12.390
early May in the pandemic,

00:20:12.390 --> 00:20:17.390
and it was sort of linearly
extrapolating these curves,

00:20:18.100 --> 00:20:20.355
Well, linearly in the law of scale.

00:20:20.355 --> 00:20:23.730
So in other words, sort of
exponentially extrapolating them,

00:20:23.730 --> 00:20:25.290
and then just sort of trying to say,

00:20:25.290 --> 00:20:28.100
okay, well, what's the
thing that happened right

00:20:28.100 --> 00:20:30.300
before some of these
curves are turning down

00:20:31.405 --> 00:20:32.413
oh, lo and behold, right?

00:20:33.291 --> 00:20:35.200
Low and behold, it's masks.

00:20:35.200 --> 00:20:40.200
So not really super rigorous way

00:20:40.760 --> 00:20:42.753
of establishing causal hypothesis.

00:20:46.520 --> 00:20:48.620
And you saw lots and lots
of studies like this,

00:20:48.620 --> 00:20:50.760
where this was really the
method that was being used.

00:20:50.760 --> 00:20:53.420
You would just get,
somebody would kind of take

00:20:53.420 --> 00:20:55.930
a particular place, in this
case, it was the Delaware,

00:20:55.930 --> 00:20:58.180
it was the Delaware
Department of Health data

00:21:01.887 --> 00:21:03.980
on infections in Delaware.

00:21:03.980 --> 00:21:07.020
And people just sort of putting

00:21:07.020 --> 00:21:08.460
what they tend to be the dates

00:21:08.460 --> 00:21:11.520
on which some of these public
health interventions happened,

00:21:11.520 --> 00:21:14.910
and then trying to line them
up with bumps in the curve

00:21:14.910 --> 00:21:16.960
where I pretty clearly see that you've got

00:21:16.960 --> 00:21:20.053
just kind of a fairly
noisy and bumpy curve here.

00:21:20.983 --> 00:21:23.520
And then just sort of trying to draw

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:29.120
causal hypothesis out of this.

00:21:29.120 --> 00:21:33.300
And this was something that
I posted it on Facebook

00:21:33.300 --> 00:21:34.583
just to make fun of this.

00:21:35.976 --> 00:21:39.810
But so I'd been in California
until on June 15th,

00:21:39.810 --> 00:21:42.930
we came back from California,
we returned to Florida,

00:21:42.930 --> 00:21:47.620
and this was a chart of the
hospitalizations in Florida.

00:21:47.620 --> 00:21:49.500
And it's very easy to just say,

00:21:49.500 --> 00:21:53.845
oh, okay, here's when
Eric returned to Florida,

00:21:53.845 --> 00:21:56.890
and look with what happened to
hospitalizations in Florida,

00:21:56.890 --> 00:21:57.740
right after that.

00:21:58.970 --> 00:22:02.340
But of course, that's not a
particularly effective way

00:22:02.340 --> 00:22:04.313
of drawing causal hypothesis.

00:22:05.410 --> 00:22:08.044
And, we were also, then I think,

00:22:08.044 --> 00:22:10.120
we saw a lot of things like this happen.

00:22:10.120 --> 00:22:12.470
So this was Scientific
American ran this story

00:22:12.470 --> 00:22:15.450
in September 15th, 2020,

00:22:15.450 --> 00:22:18.030
how New Mexico controlled
the spread of COVID-19.

00:22:18.030 --> 00:22:21.320
So we were very eager to go find places

00:22:21.320 --> 00:22:25.040
where it looked like there was success

00:22:25.040 --> 00:22:28.210
in mitigating the epidemic,
trying to figure out

00:22:28.210 --> 00:22:30.143
what did those places do,

00:22:32.037 --> 00:22:33.537
and say, oh, okay that must be,

00:22:33.537 --> 00:22:36.510
the fact that New Mexico
has controlled the spread,

00:22:36.510 --> 00:22:41.510
they must have the secret
source for making this work.

00:22:41.900 --> 00:22:46.900
And of course, this was one
of the most ill timed articles

00:22:47.280 --> 00:22:48.413
of the pandemic.

00:22:49.360 --> 00:22:51.250
You can see it here in a chart of

00:22:54.570 --> 00:22:56.330
the case rate in New Mexico, right?

00:22:56.330 --> 00:23:00.210
So here's, the article
comes out on September 15th,

00:23:00.210 --> 00:23:02.970
how New Mexico control
the spread of COVID-19,

00:23:02.970 --> 00:23:07.253
and then there's what happened
just a couple of weeks later.

00:23:09.590 --> 00:23:13.063
Similar kind of event here,

00:23:14.320 --> 00:23:18.450
here we have, this is an
article that ran in Germany,

00:23:20.930 --> 00:23:23.660
it's Hamburg proof that an emergency brake

00:23:23.660 --> 00:23:25.973
can get COVID-19 cases down.

00:23:27.100 --> 00:23:28.830
So people thought, okay, look,

00:23:28.830 --> 00:23:30.410
Hamburg is doing really well.

00:23:30.410 --> 00:23:32.610
They must be establishing something,

00:23:32.610 --> 00:23:35.820
must have some effective
method of doing this,

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:37.960
but of course, couple of things,

00:23:37.960 --> 00:23:39.930
as we've made these things, this was just,

00:23:39.930 --> 00:23:42.537
always these things tend to come out right

00:23:42.537 --> 00:23:43.410
before things would get bad,

00:23:43.410 --> 00:23:45.530
but sort of also, interestingly,

00:23:45.530 --> 00:23:47.330
it's interesting, I think to line up

00:23:49.491 --> 00:23:53.140
this is the ICU utilization
for COVID-19 patients

00:23:53.140 --> 00:23:58.140
per million population
comparing the Hamburg

00:23:58.200 --> 00:23:59.430
sort of administrative region,

00:23:59.430 --> 00:24:01.421
what they call the Bundesland,

00:24:01.421 --> 00:24:05.513
I guess the sort of Stockholm
County, Stockholm Lans.

00:24:06.559 --> 00:24:08.630
And you can see, that pretty clearly,

00:24:08.630 --> 00:24:12.760
whatever is going on in these places

00:24:12.760 --> 00:24:16.340
is something fairly regional, right?

00:24:16.340 --> 00:24:19.670
And so any attempt to kind of,

00:24:19.670 --> 00:24:22.920
look for the interventions that Hamburg

00:24:22.920 --> 00:24:25.890
was carrying out at these various places,

00:24:25.890 --> 00:24:30.853
closing restaurants, having
mask mandates, closing shops,

00:24:32.000 --> 00:24:36.610
making masks required in private cars,

00:24:36.610 --> 00:24:38.620
having curfews, et cetera.

00:24:38.620 --> 00:24:39.770
And you can sort of see,

00:24:39.770 --> 00:24:41.390
people are going through this

00:24:41.390 --> 00:24:45.140
and attributing these values
zigs and zags in the graph

00:24:45.140 --> 00:24:46.606
to all these things.

00:24:46.606 --> 00:24:51.380
But that we know that Stockholm
was not doing these things.

00:24:51.380 --> 00:24:53.740
And given that Stockholm and Hamburg are

00:24:53.740 --> 00:24:56.260
kind of proximal to each other,

00:24:56.260 --> 00:25:00.070
have similar climate are
sort of under whatever,

00:25:00.070 --> 00:25:04.120
whatever makes these curves come and go,

00:25:04.120 --> 00:25:07.140
plausibly were being affected by them

00:25:07.140 --> 00:25:09.420
in relatively similar ways.

00:25:09.420 --> 00:25:12.693
We can see that it this is the course,

00:25:12.693 --> 00:25:14.220
I don't wanna suggest that

00:25:14.220 --> 00:25:16.210
I can make better inferences myself,

00:25:16.210 --> 00:25:20.750
but it does seem sort of at
least prima fascia plausible

00:25:20.750 --> 00:25:22.300
that means that these attributions

00:25:22.300 --> 00:25:24.950
of the various zigs and zags and curve

00:25:24.950 --> 00:25:27.943
to these interventions is spurious here.

00:25:29.640 --> 00:25:34.544
We saw lots of cases like this,

00:25:34.544 --> 00:25:39.544
this was a very widely
cited and influential study

00:25:40.330 --> 00:25:45.330
by some Monica Gandhi here
is the last author on this,

00:25:45.422 --> 00:25:49.391
but I suspect probably that's
one of his last author,

00:25:49.391 --> 00:25:50.224
she was really the main author.

00:25:51.450 --> 00:25:55.207
She was a major leader
in providing evidence

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:02.730
for the effectiveness of mask mandates.

00:26:02.730 --> 00:26:06.853
So this was a paper that
posted on November 4th.

00:26:08.240 --> 00:26:11.254
And the paper was withdrawn,

00:26:11.254 --> 00:26:12.810
I forget exactly what the timeline

00:26:12.810 --> 00:26:14.410
between occupation travelers,

00:26:14.410 --> 00:26:16.864
but they withdrew it for...

00:26:16.864 --> 00:26:18.390
They say, the author said,
what's drawn was (indistinct),

00:26:18.390 --> 00:26:22.100
because there increased
cases of SARS COV two

00:26:22.100 --> 00:26:24.553
in the areas we originally analyzed.

00:26:24.553 --> 00:26:25.386
And it's like, the other thing,

00:26:25.386 --> 00:26:28.150
they've taken some places
where there were mask mandates,

00:26:28.150 --> 00:26:30.550
some places where they
were not mask mandates,

00:26:30.550 --> 00:26:32.220
they've used very statistical methods

00:26:32.220 --> 00:26:36.180
to show that the case rates
had grown in the places

00:26:36.180 --> 00:26:38.010
where there weren't mask mates,

00:26:38.010 --> 00:26:40.390
and hadn't in the places where there were.

00:26:40.390 --> 00:26:43.450
But then within a month or two,

00:26:43.450 --> 00:26:44.560
they had to withdraw paper,

00:26:44.560 --> 00:26:47.910
because many of the places
that they were claiming

00:26:47.910 --> 00:26:51.400
had been saved by the mask mandates,

00:26:51.400 --> 00:26:53.770
were just catching up with
the places that didn't

00:26:53.770 --> 00:26:56.163
have the mask mandates
a little while later.

00:26:59.619 --> 00:27:02.000
And then one of the
things I wanna talk about,

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:07.000
especially this was a paper
that came out in December, 2020,

00:27:08.060 --> 00:27:13.060
but I believe appeared in a pre performer,

00:27:15.083 --> 00:27:17.370
a couple or maybe three months earlier

00:27:17.370 --> 00:27:22.370
that used a method called
synthetic controls.

00:27:22.380 --> 00:27:24.740
I wanna talk about a little bit today

00:27:24.740 --> 00:27:27.080
to show that face masks had

00:27:27.080 --> 00:27:29.680
considerably reduced COVID-19 cases

00:27:29.680 --> 00:27:32.540
in the parts of Germany and
the Institute of Mathematics.

00:27:32.540 --> 00:27:33.373
I mean, I think, again,

00:27:33.373 --> 00:27:37.083
not that evidence kind of
seemed to evaporate over time,

00:27:38.010 --> 00:27:40.370
but I wanna talk about this a little bit,

00:27:40.370 --> 00:27:44.650
because early on we were seeing like,

00:27:44.650 --> 00:27:46.550
the Financial Times thing where

00:27:46.550 --> 00:27:49.930
just really sort of crude
causal attributions,

00:27:49.930 --> 00:27:51.370
here, look, here's a chart,

00:27:51.370 --> 00:27:54.960
this is because of the
culture of bowing or whatever,

00:27:54.960 --> 00:27:59.720
sort of really very fast and
loose inferences like that.

00:27:59.720 --> 00:28:02.570
Over time this method
of synthetic controls

00:28:02.570 --> 00:28:06.620
became sort of really quite prominent

00:28:06.620 --> 00:28:10.660
in trying to be more careful
in establishing these things.

00:28:10.660 --> 00:28:12.820
So in that paper, by the screen,

00:28:12.820 --> 00:28:16.480
you find this quite strong sentence,

00:28:16.480 --> 00:28:18.260
which has a detailed
analysis of the timing

00:28:18.260 --> 00:28:20.720
of all public health
measures in the region.

00:28:20.720 --> 00:28:23.120
We studied guarantees,
that we currently tried

00:28:23.120 --> 00:28:24.793
to our findings to face masks,

00:28:25.764 --> 00:28:27.630
and not around erroneously to
other public health measures.

00:28:27.630 --> 00:28:29.897
So we get to this in a bit,

00:28:29.897 --> 00:28:30.730
but I wanna talk a little bit about

00:28:30.730 --> 00:28:32.020
this synthetic control method,

00:28:32.020 --> 00:28:36.160
and maybe suggest reasons
why it's not quite

00:28:36.160 --> 00:28:37.950
the magic bullet that
many of the people who

00:28:37.950 --> 00:28:40.807
were trying to use it over
the last year claimed it was.

00:28:43.360 --> 00:28:46.110
This was another sort of famous pretty

00:28:46.110 --> 00:28:47.850
widely shared headline
that this was a based

00:28:47.850 --> 00:28:50.810
on a study that used the
synthetic control method,

00:28:50.810 --> 00:28:53.850
that tried to show that the
Sturgis Motorcycle Rally,

00:28:53.850 --> 00:28:58.850
which happened in South
Dakota in June, I guess,

00:28:58.860 --> 00:29:00.943
was responsible for maybe May,

00:29:01.958 --> 00:29:03.770
was responsible for a large
amount of that spread.

00:29:03.770 --> 00:29:06.520
You may remember that the upper Midwest

00:29:06.520 --> 00:29:08.780
had had its big wave right

00:29:08.780 --> 00:29:11.120
around the time of this Motorcycle Rally.

00:29:11.120 --> 00:29:13.960
So, okay.

00:29:13.960 --> 00:29:16.540
So I just wanna step back a little bit

00:29:17.472 --> 00:29:19.370
and talk about non-linear phenomena,

00:29:19.370 --> 00:29:23.780
and why, just sort of illustrate

00:29:23.780 --> 00:29:27.420
that non-linear phenomena tend
to have interesting patterns

00:29:27.420 --> 00:29:30.960
that don't really call for explanation.

00:29:30.960 --> 00:29:35.350
So this is maybe one of
the most classic examples

00:29:35.350 --> 00:29:38.000
of a non-linear phenomena,
this is a double pendulum,

00:29:38.989 --> 00:29:41.540
it's a pendulum on the
end of the pendulum.

00:29:41.540 --> 00:29:45.260
And when you watch
something like this swing,

00:29:45.260 --> 00:29:49.100
you can just see that it just goes through

00:29:49.100 --> 00:29:51.420
these moments where it
kind of spins loudly

00:29:53.220 --> 00:29:55.400
and then kind of slows down a bit,

00:29:55.400 --> 00:29:58.150
and then will swing loudly later.

00:29:58.150 --> 00:30:00.140
And anybody who watched
something like this

00:30:00.140 --> 00:30:02.420
and thought, oh, I wanna try to explain

00:30:02.420 --> 00:30:06.440
why at eight seconds into it,

00:30:06.440 --> 00:30:07.840
it starts spinning furiously,

00:30:07.840 --> 00:30:11.393
and then 12 seconds into it,
stops spinning furiously.

00:30:12.969 --> 00:30:16.690
If you had a wild imagination,

00:30:16.690 --> 00:30:19.700
you could imagine coming up
with all kinds of explanations

00:30:19.700 --> 00:30:21.530
for why this kind of thing is happening,

00:30:21.530 --> 00:30:25.843
but it's the nature of
the double pendulum,

00:30:26.929 --> 00:30:29.750
and it's part of the nature
of being nonlinear system,

00:30:29.750 --> 00:30:32.230
that it just exhibits these quite

00:30:32.230 --> 00:30:34.117
interesting sporadic things.

00:30:34.117 --> 00:30:36.310
And this is actually a simulation

00:30:36.310 --> 00:30:39.750
just because fluids are a little harder

00:30:39.750 --> 00:30:41.493
to visualize than pendulums,

00:30:41.493 --> 00:30:43.530
but you can see that's a real data too.

00:30:43.530 --> 00:30:45.750
This is so-called
Rayleigh-Bernard conduction,

00:30:45.750 --> 00:30:48.660
in our collection where you
have a hot plate on the bottom

00:30:48.660 --> 00:30:51.340
and a cold plate on the top of some fluid

00:30:52.230 --> 00:30:55.310
and pretty quickly, you can see that

00:30:55.310 --> 00:30:59.123
all kinds of interesting
patterns start to form,

00:31:00.300 --> 00:31:05.300
and anybody who was not familiar
with what's going on here,

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:07.880
and wanted to then explain why

00:31:07.880 --> 00:31:12.550
is there a dark red patch here
and a dark blue patch here?

00:31:12.550 --> 00:31:17.550
And why is this thing displaying
all the patterns like this,

00:31:17.690 --> 00:31:19.240
you can kind of get a global account

00:31:19.240 --> 00:31:22.990
of why it has the sort of general,

00:31:22.990 --> 00:31:27.938
why it's got a generally
sort of Eddy-ish structure,

00:31:27.938 --> 00:31:29.080
but if you tried to explain,

00:31:29.080 --> 00:31:31.948
why is there a big red Eddy here,

00:31:31.948 --> 00:31:33.400
and a big blue Eddy over there,

00:31:33.400 --> 00:31:38.123
you'd be pretty quickly getting
yourself into trouble here.

00:31:40.090 --> 00:31:41.427
Okay.

00:31:41.427 --> 00:31:43.650
So just a few things I think worth noting

00:31:43.650 --> 00:31:45.083
about non-linear systems,

00:31:47.010 --> 00:31:48.710
we just know this from the stay

00:31:48.710 --> 00:31:50.293
of a wide variety of systems,

00:31:52.798 --> 00:31:55.216
they can display abrupt transitions

00:31:55.216 --> 00:31:56.970
in responses to small changes, right?

00:31:56.970 --> 00:32:01.970
So if you had two fluids
next to each other,

00:32:02.710 --> 00:32:04.980
one that was exhibiting
shallow convection,

00:32:04.980 --> 00:32:08.070
and one that was
exhibiting deep convection,

00:32:08.070 --> 00:32:09.980
an inexperienced observer might think

00:32:09.980 --> 00:32:12.640
there was some deep
substantial causal factor

00:32:12.640 --> 00:32:13.980
responsible for that difference, right?

00:32:13.980 --> 00:32:16.177
They would look at these
two convective systems,

00:32:16.177 --> 00:32:17.840
and say, look they look
radically different,

00:32:17.840 --> 00:32:20.270
there must be some explanation

00:32:20.270 --> 00:32:22.780
for why this one's
undergoing deep convection,

00:32:22.780 --> 00:32:26.100
and that one is undergoing
shallow convection.

00:32:26.100 --> 00:32:29.070
But the only explanation
that there would be,

00:32:29.070 --> 00:32:31.460
would be a very microscopic,

00:32:31.460 --> 00:32:34.260
and very cognitively unsatisfied one.

00:32:34.260 --> 00:32:35.970
It would be if you could, in fact,

00:32:35.970 --> 00:32:40.410
if you really had an unbelievably
detailed logical system,

00:32:40.410 --> 00:32:41.870
you might be able to track it down

00:32:41.870 --> 00:32:43.990
to some very small difference,

00:32:43.990 --> 00:32:46.520
and identify what that
small difference was.

00:32:46.520 --> 00:32:48.560
It wouldn't be due to nothing,

00:32:48.560 --> 00:32:50.560
but it wouldn't be the
kind of thing that somebody

00:32:50.560 --> 00:32:55.560
who's experienced and he was
looking for some of that.

00:32:56.230 --> 00:32:57.240
So similarly learnt, right?

00:32:57.240 --> 00:33:02.240
We know that avalanches can be
caused to get a pile of sand,

00:33:03.520 --> 00:33:08.520
one very ill placed screen added
to an otherwise stable pile

00:33:08.750 --> 00:33:11.090
can cause a massive avalanche,

00:33:11.090 --> 00:33:13.173
perhaps more relevant to pandemics.

00:33:14.080 --> 00:33:16.480
We know that if you have
like a lot of sub nodes

00:33:17.520 --> 00:33:21.790
and you just assign random
connections between those nodes,

00:33:21.790 --> 00:33:25.010
and then you look at
how many paths there are

00:33:25.010 --> 00:33:26.750
from the top to the bottom,

00:33:26.750 --> 00:33:30.740
very tiny change in the
probability of connections,

00:33:30.740 --> 00:33:34.140
can sort of massively
give sort of big quality

00:33:34.140 --> 00:33:37.470
of changes to the number
of paths that you'll find

00:33:37.470 --> 00:33:38.950
between top and bottom.

00:33:38.950 --> 00:33:42.509
So a lot of times I sort
of take on lots in here,

00:33:42.509 --> 00:33:46.960
is that a lot of times these
kinds of behavioral changes

00:33:46.960 --> 00:33:48.840
that you see in non-linear phenomena

00:33:48.840 --> 00:33:51.250
cry out for explanation,

00:33:51.250 --> 00:33:53.540
even though the true explanation

00:33:53.540 --> 00:33:57.540
is often microscopic, nearly
impossible to identify,

00:33:57.540 --> 00:34:02.540
and I think really, I would
say cognitively unsatisfying,

00:34:02.840 --> 00:34:06.130
just in the sense that
it's not a kind of thing

00:34:06.130 --> 00:34:10.040
that as human beings, when we encounter

00:34:10.040 --> 00:34:11.380
these kinds of phenomena,

00:34:11.380 --> 00:34:13.926
it's not the kind of
thing that we typically

00:34:13.926 --> 00:34:16.903
are inclined to attribute
the explanation to.

00:34:18.608 --> 00:34:20.770
It's just a picture of Jupiter here

00:34:20.770 --> 00:34:22.040
with it's famous red spot,

00:34:22.040 --> 00:34:24.600
which is just another one of these

00:34:24.600 --> 00:34:27.420
sort of coherent structures that emerges

00:34:27.420 --> 00:34:30.120
out of a nonlinear system
that, if you wanted to know,

00:34:30.120 --> 00:34:32.216
if you lived on Jupiter and you wanna know

00:34:32.216 --> 00:34:34.860
why is the red spot here?

00:34:34.860 --> 00:34:37.610
You would not get a very
cognitively satisfying answer.

00:34:37.610 --> 00:34:39.049
Okay.

00:34:39.049 --> 00:34:42.440
So one of the main take
home lessons here at the top

00:34:42.440 --> 00:34:47.403
is when you have these
three things together,

00:34:49.567 --> 00:34:53.240
a system that exhibits a
fair degree of non-linearity,

00:34:53.240 --> 00:34:56.163
a sort of rich causal
menu available to you,

00:34:57.560 --> 00:35:00.513
think of the original, a
little Financial Times chart,

00:35:01.900 --> 00:35:04.420
who locked down when, who
has a culture of mask wearing

00:35:04.420 --> 00:35:07.500
and bowing, who had an elderly population,

00:35:07.500 --> 00:35:09.300
who was using custom trace,

00:35:09.300 --> 00:35:11.060
who was doing,

00:35:11.060 --> 00:35:12.160
So you have a sort of rich causal manual

00:35:12.160 --> 00:35:14.860
you're gonna appeal to, right?

00:35:14.860 --> 00:35:17.860
And something that, I think this

00:35:17.860 --> 00:35:22.360
is famous anthropologist
Evans Prichard noted this

00:35:22.360 --> 00:35:25.430
when he was studying the
(indistinct) in Africa, right?

00:35:25.430 --> 00:35:28.420
That's a pretty common
feature of human beings

00:35:28.420 --> 00:35:30.500
that when people get sick,

00:35:30.500 --> 00:35:32.590
or when bad things happen to people,

00:35:32.590 --> 00:35:34.590
they immediately are
looking for explanations

00:35:34.590 --> 00:35:36.260
in the form of why here, why now?

00:35:36.260 --> 00:35:38.420
Why is this happening to me,

00:35:38.420 --> 00:35:41.210
why is it happening here
not there, what's going on.

00:35:41.210 --> 00:35:44.410
I think when you get these
three things together,

00:35:44.410 --> 00:35:46.960
non-linearity, a rich causal menu

00:35:46.960 --> 00:35:50.060
that you can appeal to,
to explain those things.

00:35:50.060 --> 00:35:54.260
And a sort of urgent desire to know

00:35:54.260 --> 00:35:57.020
why is the pandemic bad
here, but not there?

00:35:57.020 --> 00:35:59.020
Why is it happening to us, but not them?

00:35:59.020 --> 00:36:02.030
Why were they saved and we're not?

00:36:02.030 --> 00:36:03.780
I think these three things together

00:36:03.780 --> 00:36:06.503
often lead to dangerous inferences.

00:36:08.060 --> 00:36:11.747
And just to give you a
little taste of this,

00:36:11.747 --> 00:36:12.860
as I started thinking
about this I was like,

00:36:12.860 --> 00:36:15.970
there's gotta be a really
good example of this

00:36:16.910 --> 00:36:18.780
from the history of science.

00:36:18.780 --> 00:36:21.140
And the thing that occurred to me as

00:36:21.140 --> 00:36:23.630
I was thinking about
different non-linear systems,

00:36:23.630 --> 00:36:27.470
earthquakes, of course, the
results of non-linear phenomena

00:36:27.470 --> 00:36:30.370
and there was a whole
industry in sort of late,

00:36:30.370 --> 00:36:33.130
mid to late 19th century
of trying to understand

00:36:33.130 --> 00:36:35.020
what caused earthquakes.

00:36:35.020 --> 00:36:37.313
Again, because earthquakes
displaying exactly

00:36:37.313 --> 00:36:42.313
this kind of clumpy non-linear based

00:36:42.650 --> 00:36:45.723
kind of dispersion of depth.

00:36:46.900 --> 00:36:50.550
So you got all these people in
the late 19th century arguing

00:36:50.550 --> 00:36:54.026
about, is it due to atmosphere pressure?

00:36:54.026 --> 00:36:55.500
Is it due to the seasons?

00:36:55.500 --> 00:36:57.360
Is it due to the position of the moon?

00:36:57.360 --> 00:37:00.610
Is it due to the position of the sun?

00:37:00.610 --> 00:37:03.550
And I think much like
many of these things,

00:37:03.550 --> 00:37:06.560
not only do you, in these cases have

00:37:06.560 --> 00:37:09.240
this kind of record data with all kinds

00:37:09.240 --> 00:37:11.947
of you have a large causal menu, right?

00:37:11.947 --> 00:37:13.610
All these people are like looking at what,

00:37:13.610 --> 00:37:14.807
they wanna know what causes earthquakes.

00:37:14.807 --> 00:37:17.010
And they immediately imagined

00:37:17.010 --> 00:37:19.290
these are the things that
might be causing responsible,

00:37:19.290 --> 00:37:22.095
they're not the plausible,
atmosphere pressure,

00:37:22.095 --> 00:37:24.330
position of the moon,
position of the sun, tides,

00:37:24.330 --> 00:37:25.680
things like that.

00:37:25.680 --> 00:37:28.430
They have this messy clumpy data,

00:37:28.430 --> 00:37:30.990
and then of course what we now know too,

00:37:30.990 --> 00:37:33.990
is that their data were bad.

00:37:33.990 --> 00:37:36.580
First of all, they didn't
know, as we now know

00:37:36.580 --> 00:37:38.360
that most of the earthquakes on the planet

00:37:38.360 --> 00:37:40.410
happen underneath the oceans.

00:37:40.410 --> 00:37:43.493
So they were missing at
least 70% of the earthquakes.

00:37:44.860 --> 00:37:48.650
And you get a lot of disagreement

00:37:48.650 --> 00:37:51.710
between the scientists that all boils down

00:37:51.710 --> 00:37:53.590
to they're looking at
different data, right?

00:37:53.590 --> 00:37:56.471
So you have one person
saying, "Oh, you know,

00:37:56.471 --> 00:37:58.940
"it's clearly the position of the moon."

00:37:58.940 --> 00:38:01.707
And then someone else using
their data saying, "No, look,

00:38:01.707 --> 00:38:04.647
"I can clearly show that
there's no relationship

00:38:04.647 --> 00:38:08.167
"between the moon and earthquakes."

00:38:11.450 --> 00:38:12.283
Okay.

00:38:12.283 --> 00:38:14.523
But these were 19th century rubes,

00:38:15.390 --> 00:38:18.570
we have much better data than they had.

00:38:18.570 --> 00:38:22.040
We have much better methods
of inference than they do,

00:38:22.040 --> 00:38:23.423
surely we can do better.

00:38:25.701 --> 00:38:29.440
And surely we can identify the causes

00:38:29.440 --> 00:38:31.490
of differential outcomes in the pandemic.

00:38:32.970 --> 00:38:36.120
Well, a number of things
I think are finally here.

00:38:36.120 --> 00:38:39.230
So I just wanna show you a
nice little picture that I like

00:38:39.230 --> 00:38:43.080
this is based on, so Oxford
has been running this

00:38:45.440 --> 00:38:47.570
since pretty early in the pandemic.

00:38:47.570 --> 00:38:49.440
They've been tracking what they call

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:53.510
a stringent fee index
for different places.

00:38:53.510 --> 00:38:56.260
That's meant to be a sort
of one number summary

00:38:56.260 --> 00:38:59.030
of how aggressive public health measures

00:38:59.030 --> 00:39:01.030
have been in various places in trying

00:39:01.030 --> 00:39:03.650
to control the pandemic.

00:39:03.650 --> 00:39:08.283
And you can plot them here
for at least the US states.

00:39:09.210 --> 00:39:13.630
So, here on the horizontal axis,

00:39:13.630 --> 00:39:15.480
you have this Oxford stringency index

00:39:18.083 --> 00:39:20.063
averaged over March, 2020 to May, 2021.

00:39:20.990 --> 00:39:24.860
Here you have COVID-19 deaths, 400,000,

00:39:24.860 --> 00:39:27.003
The size of the blob is the,

00:39:27.860 --> 00:39:29.850
so California's the biggest blob.

00:39:29.850 --> 00:39:31.260
Texas is nice and big.

00:39:31.260 --> 00:39:32.803
Hawaii is a tiny little dot.

00:39:34.548 --> 00:39:35.410
The red states are red.

00:39:35.410 --> 00:39:36.800
The blue states are blue.

00:39:36.800 --> 00:39:37.900
The purple states are purple.

00:39:37.900 --> 00:39:39.300
We all know what that means.

00:39:40.757 --> 00:39:42.050
And you know, if you look at this,

00:39:42.050 --> 00:39:43.510
at least to the naked eye, it looks like

00:39:43.510 --> 00:39:45.770
maybe there's some
interesting patterns here,

00:39:45.770 --> 00:39:50.550
maybe there's some degree
of covariance here,

00:39:50.550 --> 00:39:53.440
but it's not entirely obvious,

00:39:53.440 --> 00:39:56.130
but what's interesting,
is the various ways

00:39:56.130 --> 00:40:00.280
that people can kind
of cherry pick examples

00:40:00.280 --> 00:40:02.840
and make the cherry
picking with plausible.

00:40:02.840 --> 00:40:04.940
So this is, I think you saw a number like,

00:40:06.498 --> 00:40:07.331
we heard a lot about this about,

00:40:07.331 --> 00:40:09.542
people arguing about Sweden a lot

00:40:09.542 --> 00:40:10.375
over the course of the pandemic,

00:40:10.375 --> 00:40:11.650
and the argument was always right.

00:40:11.650 --> 00:40:14.270
Well, what's the relevant
comparator for Sweden?

00:40:14.270 --> 00:40:15.850
Is it the European average,

00:40:15.850 --> 00:40:18.790
or is it, you know,
it's Scandinavian peers?

00:40:18.790 --> 00:40:22.400
And just sort of how you
decide that question,

00:40:22.400 --> 00:40:24.107
is gonna determine what you think

00:40:24.107 --> 00:40:25.440
the (indistinct) of Sweden is.

00:40:25.440 --> 00:40:27.340
If you think the relevant
comparative for Sweden,

00:40:27.340 --> 00:40:29.420
is its Scandinavian peers,

00:40:29.420 --> 00:40:31.680
well then looks like Sweden was a disaster

00:40:31.680 --> 00:40:33.140
relative to its peers.

00:40:33.140 --> 00:40:34.560
If you think Sweden's comparator

00:40:34.560 --> 00:40:37.770
ought to be European union as a whole,

00:40:37.770 --> 00:40:39.770
Sweden's kind of in
the middle of the path,

00:40:39.770 --> 00:40:42.280
despite having not really, you know,

00:40:42.280 --> 00:40:45.590
Sweden would be very low
on this stringent index.

00:40:45.590 --> 00:40:47.770
I forget what I should
have looked up there

00:40:47.770 --> 00:40:49.327
with their actual volume of (indistinct).

00:40:50.463 --> 00:40:52.290
I guess it's down here somewhere

00:40:52.290 --> 00:40:54.290
in like Missouri or something like that.

00:40:55.180 --> 00:40:57.860
But then here's what's interesting, right?

00:40:57.860 --> 00:41:00.680
If you kind of play this
game of thinking that,

00:41:00.680 --> 00:41:03.000
you know what the relevant
peer comparators are,

00:41:03.000 --> 00:41:06.024
you can show all kinds
of interesting things.

00:41:06.024 --> 00:41:07.470
So if you pick North
Dakota and South Lake,

00:41:07.470 --> 00:41:09.440
well, surely those are good comparators,

00:41:09.440 --> 00:41:11.498
okay, well, they don't really have

00:41:11.498 --> 00:41:12.400
the very different stringency index,

00:41:12.400 --> 00:41:14.442
but to the extent that they do,

00:41:14.442 --> 00:41:17.680
it looks like stringency
led to more deaths.

00:41:17.680 --> 00:41:20.200
Maybe you think what about
the Pacific Northwest?

00:41:20.200 --> 00:41:22.150
How about Oregon and Washington?

00:41:22.150 --> 00:41:25.100
Again, you can find the opposite of what

00:41:25.100 --> 00:41:27.107
you would think is the
prima facie (indistinct).

00:41:29.360 --> 00:41:32.170
You can pick Alabama and Mississippi,

00:41:32.170 --> 00:41:34.130
pretty clear those are peers, right?

00:41:34.130 --> 00:41:37.865
So the point here is like,
you see a lot of this,

00:41:37.865 --> 00:41:38.698
I think people play a lot of this game

00:41:38.698 --> 00:41:40.860
of like, oh, well, we can figure out

00:41:40.860 --> 00:41:43.240
what the relevant peers here are,

00:41:43.240 --> 00:41:45.600
and in the process of
that, we can make this.

00:41:45.600 --> 00:41:49.264
But again, this is just
kind of a cautionary tale,

00:41:49.264 --> 00:41:51.150
and this is a kind of dangerous game,

00:41:51.150 --> 00:41:54.710
if you don't know how
much people are sort of

00:41:54.710 --> 00:41:58.260
loading the dice when they
choose these comparators,

00:41:58.260 --> 00:42:01.560
people are liable to be able
to demonstrate anything,

00:42:01.560 --> 00:42:06.230
anything they want with
comparisons like that.

00:42:06.230 --> 00:42:07.300
Okay.

00:42:07.300 --> 00:42:11.610
So what are the sort of
most sophisticated methods

00:42:11.610 --> 00:42:13.110
that we've had available to us

00:42:14.010 --> 00:42:15.270
over the course of the pandemic,

00:42:15.270 --> 00:42:20.270
for identifying what are
the causal determinants

00:42:21.080 --> 00:42:24.233
of different outcomes that
we've seen around the world?

00:42:25.543 --> 00:42:27.910
I should put them into two categories,

00:42:27.910 --> 00:42:30.600
we've had sort of sophisticated
statistical methods,

00:42:30.600 --> 00:42:33.820
and then we've seen some
nice chance to use modeling.

00:42:33.820 --> 00:42:36.900
So it's kind of some things
like the Imperial College Model

00:42:36.900 --> 00:42:39.460
that we're originally intended to forecast

00:42:39.460 --> 00:42:42.260
what the pandemic would do,

00:42:42.260 --> 00:42:44.660
being used retrospectively to explain,

00:42:44.660 --> 00:42:46.560
why they did the things that they did.

00:42:47.630 --> 00:42:48.879
Okay.

00:42:48.879 --> 00:42:50.013
So statistical methods.

00:42:51.240 --> 00:42:54.190
Here's the game, you wanna know,

00:42:54.190 --> 00:42:58.450
did an intervention have
some causal outcome,

00:42:58.450 --> 00:43:01.740
so, you wanna know, you know,

00:43:01.740 --> 00:43:03.010
when kids go to private school,

00:43:03.010 --> 00:43:04.410
and they're better at Math,

00:43:04.410 --> 00:43:07.010
the kids who go to private
schools do better on Math tests.

00:43:07.010 --> 00:43:07.843
Okay.

00:43:07.843 --> 00:43:09.560
So what's the simplest thing you can do?

00:43:09.560 --> 00:43:11.876
You can look at kids
who go to private school

00:43:11.876 --> 00:43:12.750
and kids who don't go to private school,

00:43:12.750 --> 00:43:14.250
and see whether the kids

00:43:14.250 --> 00:43:16.483
in private school do better at Math,

00:43:16.483 --> 00:43:17.420
but obviously, what would you find?

00:43:17.420 --> 00:43:18.260
You probably find, oh yeah,

00:43:18.260 --> 00:43:19.800
the kids private school do better,

00:43:19.800 --> 00:43:23.310
but It would be because
those kids were wealthy,

00:43:23.310 --> 00:43:25.340
and wealthy kids on average,

00:43:25.340 --> 00:43:28.490
like tend to have a lot more other

00:43:28.490 --> 00:43:29.930
educational opportunities,

00:43:29.930 --> 00:43:32.850
and they tend to have a lot of things

00:43:32.850 --> 00:43:35.140
that inhibit their educational growth,

00:43:35.140 --> 00:43:37.400
like not having healthy breakfast,

00:43:37.400 --> 00:43:41.170
or, you know, having, you know,

00:43:41.170 --> 00:43:45.105
but the obvious point that
there are lots of things

00:43:45.105 --> 00:43:48.540
that could cause wealth to
lead to better Math scores.

00:43:48.540 --> 00:43:49.970
Okay.

00:43:49.970 --> 00:43:51.900
So obviously you can't just look

00:43:51.900 --> 00:43:54.450
at whether kids in private
schools are doing better

00:43:54.450 --> 00:43:56.400
than kids in public schools.

00:43:56.400 --> 00:43:58.270
Well, you'd ideally
like to do those right.

00:43:58.270 --> 00:44:01.130
take a whole bunch of kids
and randomly assign them

00:44:01.130 --> 00:44:03.470
to private school and public school,

00:44:03.470 --> 00:44:05.080
but you can't do that.

00:44:05.080 --> 00:44:07.410
You can't do that, I mean,
well, you could do that maybe,

00:44:07.410 --> 00:44:09.150
but nobody's done this,

00:44:09.150 --> 00:44:11.860
probably a lot of people
that raise apple flags,

00:44:11.860 --> 00:44:13.220
if you tried to do this.

00:44:13.220 --> 00:44:14.600
So what you can hope to
do is you can sort of

00:44:14.600 --> 00:44:17.090
hope to wait for natural experiments.

00:44:17.090 --> 00:44:19.700
So maybe you can wait until, I don't know,

00:44:19.700 --> 00:44:24.040
Cleveland one day, decides it needs to do

00:44:24.040 --> 00:44:26.820
a specialist remediation
of public schools.

00:44:26.820 --> 00:44:28.660
And so it hands out
vouchers to all the kids

00:44:28.660 --> 00:44:30.060
to go to private school,

00:44:30.060 --> 00:44:32.590
then you can maybe look and see well,

00:44:32.590 --> 00:44:34.457
did the kids who got
vouchers in Cleveland,

00:44:34.457 --> 00:44:36.433
did their Math scores suddenly go up?

00:44:38.310 --> 00:44:39.450
Probably pretty effective,

00:44:39.450 --> 00:44:41.750
but you can imagine
somebody having the worry.

00:44:41.750 --> 00:44:44.600
Well, I don't know,
maybe it just so happened

00:44:44.600 --> 00:44:48.790
that at the time that these
vouchers were being given out,

00:44:48.790 --> 00:44:51.310
maybe it just so happened that everyone

00:44:51.310 --> 00:44:52.260
was getting better at Math.

00:44:52.260 --> 00:44:55.180
Maybe that was the exact time

00:44:55.180 --> 00:44:58.430
when the school house
rock episode came on TV

00:44:58.430 --> 00:45:00.050
that was making everybody better at Math

00:45:00.050 --> 00:45:01.590
all at the same time.

00:45:01.590 --> 00:45:04.460
So how do we know that it's the vouchers

00:45:04.460 --> 00:45:06.970
and the kids going to private
schools that are doing it?

00:45:06.970 --> 00:45:08.810
Well, here's ideally
what you'd love to do,

00:45:08.810 --> 00:45:10.540
you'd love to be able to say, okay,

00:45:10.540 --> 00:45:12.100
let's look at the county.

00:45:12.100 --> 00:45:14.500
I think it's Cuyahoga County,

00:45:14.500 --> 00:45:16.519
I can never pronounce that right,

00:45:16.519 --> 00:45:19.190
Cuyahoga County, which is
where Cleveland is in like

00:45:20.649 --> 00:45:21.482
probably (indistinct) that,

00:45:22.357 --> 00:45:24.670
and maybe hopefully you could look at

00:45:24.670 --> 00:45:27.503
when the vouchers get handed out,

00:45:28.519 --> 00:45:31.683
to the public school kids in Cleveland,

00:45:32.870 --> 00:45:35.933
you could compare, you're not just looking

00:45:35.933 --> 00:45:37.120
at whether they're doing better at Math,

00:45:37.120 --> 00:45:39.590
but you could look at how much
better they're doing at Math

00:45:39.590 --> 00:45:41.350
compared to how the kids are doing

00:45:41.350 --> 00:45:42.750
in the suburbs of Cleveland.

00:45:43.620 --> 00:45:45.090
So the hope would be there,

00:45:45.090 --> 00:45:47.180
if it's not the vouchers that are making

00:45:47.180 --> 00:45:48.670
the kids do better Math,

00:45:48.670 --> 00:45:51.423
but it's some like
underlying trend in the data,

00:45:52.310 --> 00:45:54.943
by comparing how much better
they're doing in Cleveland

00:45:54.943 --> 00:45:57.450
than they are doing in the suburbs,

00:45:57.450 --> 00:46:02.200
You'd be teasing out exactly
what the contribution

00:46:02.200 --> 00:46:04.313
of the going to private school is.

00:46:05.200 --> 00:46:06.440
And this is what's known

00:46:06.440 --> 00:46:08.390
as the difference in difference method.

00:46:09.290 --> 00:46:11.260
Both Cleveland and its suburbs

00:46:11.260 --> 00:46:14.240
have some non-stationary
data, it's changing over time.

00:46:14.240 --> 00:46:15.670
There's some difference,

00:46:15.670 --> 00:46:17.840
but you're looking for the
difference in that difference.

00:46:17.840 --> 00:46:19.540
You're looking for how much did

00:46:19.540 --> 00:46:22.530
the Cleveland Math scores change,

00:46:22.530 --> 00:46:24.850
relative to what the overall trend was

00:46:24.850 --> 00:46:26.100
in the county as a whole?

00:46:28.120 --> 00:46:31.170
But crucially what this requires

00:46:31.170 --> 00:46:35.860
is that overall you have
what's known as the park side.

00:46:35.860 --> 00:46:37.942
Overall you have what's known

00:46:37.942 --> 00:46:39.600
as the parallel trends assumption,

00:46:39.600 --> 00:46:44.600
it requires that whatever
is going on in Cleveland

00:46:45.390 --> 00:46:48.560
and its suburbs is the same,

00:46:48.560 --> 00:46:53.560
except for the introduction
of the vouchers

00:46:54.580 --> 00:46:55.980
to go to the private school.

00:46:57.400 --> 00:46:58.233
Okay.

00:46:59.580 --> 00:47:01.840
Synthetic control methods are not really

00:47:01.840 --> 00:47:04.026
that interestingly different

00:47:04.026 --> 00:47:05.323
from difference in difference methods.

00:47:06.440 --> 00:47:07.830
When you first read about anything,

00:47:07.830 --> 00:47:09.500
this is like the sort of magic bullet

00:47:09.500 --> 00:47:13.860
that enables us to do
what might be harder,

00:47:13.860 --> 00:47:18.080
because sometimes it's hard
to find comparators there

00:47:19.300 --> 00:47:22.090
where parallel trends are in place.

00:47:22.090 --> 00:47:25.310
So what synthetic control method does

00:47:25.310 --> 00:47:28.930
is rather than comparing the place

00:47:28.930 --> 00:47:30.783
where the intervention happens,

00:47:31.830 --> 00:47:36.530
to some particular
comparator use something

00:47:36.530 --> 00:47:39.753
like machine learning to blow
the synthetic comparator.

00:47:40.650 --> 00:47:42.600
So here, just this nice little example.

00:47:43.750 --> 00:47:46.080
Here's this place Salta,

00:47:46.080 --> 00:47:49.790
that's introducing a new tourism policy.

00:47:49.790 --> 00:47:52.260
It's trying to determine
whether that tourism policy

00:47:52.260 --> 00:47:55.563
is leading to increased
employment in the tourism sector.

00:47:56.630 --> 00:47:57.940
And all these little groups,

00:47:57.940 --> 00:48:01.640
so here's when the intervention happens

00:48:01.640 --> 00:48:03.670
on the dotted line, right?

00:48:03.670 --> 00:48:08.490
Here's a black line is Salta's
tourism employment level.

00:48:08.490 --> 00:48:13.490
And these gray lines are just a whole set

00:48:14.500 --> 00:48:16.700
of comprehend comparable places

00:48:17.800 --> 00:48:20.620
that didn't have a change
in their trust and policy.

00:48:20.620 --> 00:48:23.150
And when you do this, you try to find

00:48:24.982 --> 00:48:29.982
a subset of these places,

00:48:30.270 --> 00:48:32.880
and a weaving for them that produces

00:48:32.880 --> 00:48:35.080
this synthetic comparator here,

00:48:35.080 --> 00:48:37.230
and that's what this
little dotted line is.

00:48:38.602 --> 00:48:40.440
And then the hope is that
this little dotted line

00:48:40.440 --> 00:48:43.360
will be very close to what Salta was like

00:48:43.360 --> 00:48:44.970
prior to the intervention.

00:48:44.970 --> 00:48:48.820
And then if that's the case,
maybe you can attribute

00:48:48.820 --> 00:48:51.370
whatever difference happens, sorry,

00:48:51.370 --> 00:48:55.040
whatever difference happens afterward

00:48:55.040 --> 00:48:58.403
as being attributable
to the tourism policy.

00:48:59.490 --> 00:49:04.427
But crucially the parallel
trends still need to be in place.

00:49:05.479 --> 00:49:06.312
Okay.

00:49:06.312 --> 00:49:09.078
Parallel trends still need to be in place,

00:49:09.078 --> 00:49:14.060
you've been able to make
yourself a synthetic control

00:49:14.970 --> 00:49:19.970
that looks a lot like your
target area private intervention,

00:49:21.320 --> 00:49:25.470
but in a way, the synthetic
control method enables you

00:49:25.470 --> 00:49:28.530
to hide differences that were there,

00:49:28.530 --> 00:49:32.740
that look like you try to
get almost a perfect match.

00:49:32.740 --> 00:49:36.006
That might be just because
the machine learning method

00:49:36.006 --> 00:49:39.030
is a really good way of data
mining for a perfect match,

00:49:39.030 --> 00:49:44.030
even in spite of an
absence of parallel trends.

00:49:45.080 --> 00:49:46.389
Okay.

00:49:46.389 --> 00:49:50.040
So this is nice paper
that was written right

00:49:50.040 --> 00:49:54.210
before the pandemic started
by one of the developers

00:49:54.210 --> 00:49:56.130
of the synthetic control method.

00:49:56.130 --> 00:49:59.400
I just wanna read you a
couple of passages from it.

00:49:59.400 --> 00:50:02.240
It says synthetic controls
provide many practical advantages

00:50:02.240 --> 00:50:04.450
for the estimation effects,
policy interventions,

00:50:04.450 --> 00:50:05.790
and other events of interest.

00:50:05.790 --> 00:50:08.350
However, like any other
statistical procedure

00:50:08.350 --> 00:50:11.640
and especially for those aimed
at estimated causal effects,

00:50:11.640 --> 00:50:13.831
the credibility of the
results depends crucially

00:50:13.831 --> 00:50:14.664
on the level of diligence exerted

00:50:14.664 --> 00:50:16.481
in the application on the method,

00:50:16.481 --> 00:50:17.650
and on whether contextual
and data requirements

00:50:17.650 --> 00:50:21.093
are met in the empirical
application at hand.

00:50:21.093 --> 00:50:22.050
And this article emphasize the notion

00:50:22.050 --> 00:50:24.400
that mechanical applications
of synthetic controls

00:50:24.400 --> 00:50:25.360
that do not take into account

00:50:25.360 --> 00:50:27.150
the context of the investigation,

00:50:27.150 --> 00:50:30.710
or the nature of the data
are risky enterprises.

00:50:30.710 --> 00:50:32.410
And that'll be in particular identifies

00:50:32.410 --> 00:50:37.410
to sort of crucial visitor
honor that needs to be in place

00:50:39.480 --> 00:50:42.860
for the synthetic control method to work.

00:50:42.860 --> 00:50:47.270
On the one hand, it's advisable to select

00:50:47.270 --> 00:50:48.430
for the donor pool,

00:50:48.430 --> 00:50:51.080
that's the bunch of places
you're gonna machine learn

00:50:52.380 --> 00:50:54.790
synthetic control out of affected

00:50:54.790 --> 00:50:57.683
by the same regional economic
shocks, is economist,

00:50:58.537 --> 00:51:00.020
as the unit where the
intervention happens.

00:51:00.020 --> 00:51:03.810
On the other hand, if spillover
effects are substantial

00:51:03.810 --> 00:51:06.760
and affect units in close
geographical proximity,

00:51:06.760 --> 00:51:08.660
those units may come up biased estimate

00:51:08.660 --> 00:51:10.920
of the counterfactual
outcome without intervention

00:51:10.920 --> 00:51:14.220
for the unit affected by the intervention.

00:51:14.220 --> 00:51:16.320
So think of it this way,

00:51:16.320 --> 00:51:19.790
you wanna know, let's say, Jena

00:51:19.790 --> 00:51:23.780
this was the mask study in
Germany (indistinct) they chose,

00:51:23.780 --> 00:51:26.980
Jena as a place where this
mask mandate was introduced,

00:51:26.980 --> 00:51:29.980
you wonder whether the
mask mandate led people

00:51:29.980 --> 00:51:33.920
to like cases to go down, okay.

00:51:33.920 --> 00:51:36.120
But you're worried about
the following thing,

00:51:36.120 --> 00:51:39.620
it's plausible that when a
unit introduces mask mandates,

00:51:39.620 --> 00:51:42.340
places around in the proximity of Jena,

00:51:42.340 --> 00:51:43.850
people will start wearing masks,

00:51:43.850 --> 00:51:48.440
'cause they see their close
relatives living in Jena.

00:51:48.440 --> 00:51:51.550
they have friends in Jena,
they commute to Jena,

00:51:51.550 --> 00:51:53.073
and some of them come back.

00:51:53.962 --> 00:51:56.620
And so you get spillover
effect of mask wearing,

00:51:56.620 --> 00:51:58.220
in the region surrounding Jena.

00:52:00.020 --> 00:52:03.260
At the same time, you wanna make sure

00:52:03.260 --> 00:52:08.260
that you are sampling from
someplace regionally close

00:52:09.210 --> 00:52:12.510
because we know in retrospect,

00:52:12.510 --> 00:52:15.720
if you think back to that sort
of Stockholm Hamburg chart

00:52:15.720 --> 00:52:17.110
that I showed you,

00:52:17.110 --> 00:52:22.110
we know that parallel
trends wildly breaks down,

00:52:23.803 --> 00:52:26.000
as you get geographically far away,

00:52:26.000 --> 00:52:30.750
if you compare Stockholm and Hamburg,

00:52:33.670 --> 00:52:36.510
even though maybe
Stockholm was much closer

00:52:36.510 --> 00:52:39.030
to let's say Florida in terms of what

00:52:39.030 --> 00:52:41.940
the public health interventions
going on in there were,

00:52:41.940 --> 00:52:43.520
you're gonna see a lot more similarities

00:52:43.520 --> 00:52:45.760
between the Stockholm and Hamburg,

00:52:45.760 --> 00:52:48.760
Stockholm and Florida
for whatever reasons,

00:52:48.760 --> 00:52:50.935
I don't wanna pretend
to know what they are,

00:52:50.935 --> 00:52:53.480
for whatever reasons
climate networks spread,

00:52:53.480 --> 00:52:56.930
whatever it is, whatever
causes geographic distance

00:52:56.930 --> 00:52:59.170
to result in different outcomes.

00:52:59.170 --> 00:53:01.087
You're gonna see different things there.

00:53:01.087 --> 00:53:03.130
So you kind of have these two desiderata,

00:53:03.130 --> 00:53:07.190
and they really, I think
in epidemics anyway,

00:53:07.190 --> 00:53:10.100
they really fundamentally conflict.

00:53:10.100 --> 00:53:12.370
Because one that I wanna make sure

00:53:17.290 --> 00:53:19.040
that the members of my donor pool

00:53:19.040 --> 00:53:21.720
are not being affected
by spillover effects.

00:53:21.720 --> 00:53:23.260
I wanna make sure that my donor pool

00:53:23.260 --> 00:53:27.160
that I'm comparing Jena to
does not include any places

00:53:27.160 --> 00:53:29.740
where the residents would be seeing

00:53:29.740 --> 00:53:32.940
the people in the unit putting masks on

00:53:32.940 --> 00:53:34.520
and wearing masks themselves,

00:53:34.520 --> 00:53:36.440
but I also wanna make sure that

00:53:36.440 --> 00:53:38.350
whatever region I'm looking at

00:53:38.350 --> 00:53:41.200
is geographically closed enough,

00:53:41.200 --> 00:53:43.890
that the kind of thing that we expect

00:53:43.890 --> 00:53:46.473
from non-linear phenomena
where there's just,

00:53:46.473 --> 00:53:49.430
clumpiness in the data causing
waves to appear regionally

00:53:49.430 --> 00:53:53.030
and all kinds of ways we
can't hope to understand,

00:53:53.030 --> 00:53:55.763
certainly not in immediate
ray as they're happening,

00:53:56.720 --> 00:53:59.360
so we need to be careful about that.

00:53:59.360 --> 00:54:04.040
And then timing is also crucial in this.

00:54:04.040 --> 00:54:06.640
So if you go to look for
example, at the Jena paper,

00:54:07.502 --> 00:54:08.960
when they sort of put up at the end of

00:54:08.960 --> 00:54:10.430
they ran the study at first,

00:54:10.430 --> 00:54:13.964
and they didn't find the
effect that we're looking for,

00:54:13.964 --> 00:54:14.950
And then they said, oh, well, actually

00:54:14.950 --> 00:54:18.520
it's because we didn't
realize at first that

00:54:18.520 --> 00:54:21.040
when you have a mask mandate,

00:54:21.040 --> 00:54:23.230
and the government announces
that the mask mandate

00:54:23.230 --> 00:54:25.377
is gonna come into place,
people will start wearing

00:54:25.377 --> 00:54:27.383
the masks before the mandate occurs.

00:54:28.400 --> 00:54:30.160
So immediately now you've introduced

00:54:30.160 --> 00:54:31.580
to the grid of flexibility,

00:54:31.580 --> 00:54:36.580
and it's no longer obvious exactly

00:54:36.750 --> 00:54:38.940
when this dotted line is happening.

00:54:38.940 --> 00:54:43.940
And if you allow yourself some flexibility

00:54:44.120 --> 00:54:48.130
in deciding when the dotted line happens,

00:54:49.560 --> 00:54:52.660
then you really got
yourself into a situation

00:54:52.660 --> 00:54:54.060
where you have a tremendous,

00:54:54.903 --> 00:54:56.426
what I would call a Duherm problem.

00:54:56.426 --> 00:54:57.680
Duherm was the guy that taught us

00:54:57.680 --> 00:55:02.680
that when you're trying to
test the scientific hypothesis,

00:55:02.940 --> 00:55:07.410
it's a bad thing, if there
are too many flexible ultra,

00:55:07.410 --> 00:55:09.450
auxiliary hypothesis that you need

00:55:09.450 --> 00:55:11.850
to connect the data to your hypothesis.

00:55:11.850 --> 00:55:15.050
So if you have too much
flexibility in deciding

00:55:15.050 --> 00:55:18.853
what the actual impactful
date of a mask mandate is,

00:55:19.690 --> 00:55:22.470
that's gonna really sort of
hurt the inferential power

00:55:22.470 --> 00:55:23.800
of your study, okay.

00:55:23.800 --> 00:55:26.433
I wanna kind of wrap
up here pretty quickly.

00:55:26.433 --> 00:55:29.380
So I'm gonna skip a couple of things here,

00:55:29.380 --> 00:55:32.703
and just kind of get
to my conclusion here.

00:55:34.370 --> 00:55:36.423
Here's I think my main conclusion, right?

00:55:37.511 --> 00:55:40.560
So non-linear phenomena often
create illusions of patterns

00:55:40.560 --> 00:55:42.213
that require explanation.

00:55:43.070 --> 00:55:45.780
I think we saw this both in
the case of the warming hiatus

00:55:45.780 --> 00:55:46.957
in climate science.

00:55:46.957 --> 00:55:49.550
And we saw this happen in
sort of week after week,

00:55:49.550 --> 00:55:52.360
month after month, over
the course of the pandemic,

00:55:52.360 --> 00:55:57.360
people sort of trying to
make causal inference,

00:55:58.040 --> 00:56:00.927
in the UA as things are going on,

00:56:00.927 --> 00:56:03.093
as the phenomena themselves are unfolding.

00:56:04.976 --> 00:56:07.420
And I think that's a specially
sort of epistemically rescue,

00:56:07.420 --> 00:56:09.540
when you have these
normally in your phenomena,

00:56:09.540 --> 00:56:11.410
and creating these patterns

00:56:11.410 --> 00:56:13.980
that have the illusion
of a prior explanation,

00:56:13.980 --> 00:56:17.210
and you're trying to do the
causal inference on the fly,

00:56:17.210 --> 00:56:22.210
and you don't have the requisite.

00:56:22.550 --> 00:56:26.720
If you think about the thing
that Abi is telling us about

00:56:26.720 --> 00:56:29.380
the synthetic control method, right?

00:56:29.380 --> 00:56:31.496
He's basically warning you,

00:56:31.496 --> 00:56:33.230
if you don't have a good handle

00:56:33.230 --> 00:56:36.360
on what's causing background noise,

00:56:36.360 --> 00:56:38.300
and causing zigging and zagging,

00:56:38.300 --> 00:56:39.650
you cannot use this method.

00:56:40.906 --> 00:56:44.536
And I think pretty obvious
in retrospect at least,

00:56:44.536 --> 00:56:46.040
that we just didn't have that kind of grip

00:56:46.040 --> 00:56:50.900
on what was making the various features

00:56:50.900 --> 00:56:55.900
of the data about those
inferences be present.

00:56:58.090 --> 00:57:01.070
So, we just didn't really
have the opportunity

00:57:01.070 --> 00:57:03.400
to have the kind of grip on the data

00:57:03.400 --> 00:57:05.690
that Abi is warning us
there that we have to have

00:57:05.690 --> 00:57:08.040
to use these kinds of
causal inference methods.

00:57:08.910 --> 00:57:13.380
And I think, this is
especially epistemically risky

00:57:13.380 --> 00:57:15.550
when the decisions that
are being supported

00:57:15.550 --> 00:57:18.210
by those differences are
particularly high stakes.

00:57:18.210 --> 00:57:21.670
And I don't need to argue for the claim

00:57:21.670 --> 00:57:23.900
that we made a lot of decisions

00:57:23.900 --> 00:57:25.770
over the course of the pandemic

00:57:25.770 --> 00:57:27.430
that were pretty high stakes.

00:57:27.430 --> 00:57:28.947
Okay, great.

00:57:28.947 --> 00:57:31.373
Thank you all, I think I'll stop there.

00:57:34.520 --> 00:57:38.650
- Thanks so much, Eric for
that wonderful presentation.

00:57:38.650 --> 00:57:41.100
Dissecting many of the plots and graphs

00:57:41.100 --> 00:57:44.840
that we've all been witnessing
over the past few months,

00:57:44.840 --> 00:57:49.840
and puzzled over how do you
sort of policy decisions

00:57:50.170 --> 00:57:53.660
were supposedly guided
entirely by the data,

00:57:53.660 --> 00:57:57.130
but it's quite clear that
the underlying complex

00:57:57.130 --> 00:57:59.660
non-linearities make it not so simple

00:57:59.660 --> 00:58:04.480
to go from the data to policy decisions.

00:58:04.480 --> 00:58:06.790
And I think the point that you were making

00:58:06.790 --> 00:58:11.790
about the masking was
particularly interesting,

00:58:12.150 --> 00:58:17.150
because of how initially I had gathered

00:58:17.480 --> 00:58:22.360
that the not emphasizing masking

00:58:22.360 --> 00:58:23.850
had to do with the fact that there

00:58:23.850 --> 00:58:27.100
was a shortage of N 95 masks,

00:58:27.100 --> 00:58:29.170
because the public was buying them up

00:58:29.170 --> 00:58:32.910
when medical staff needed
it, healthcare workers,

00:58:32.910 --> 00:58:35.600
frontline healthcare workers were,

00:58:35.600 --> 00:58:37.330
there were shortages for them.

00:58:37.330 --> 00:58:38.500
So it's very interesting how

00:58:38.500 --> 00:58:42.970
all these kinds of
external factors play in

00:58:42.970 --> 00:58:45.740
along with what the data is telling us,

00:58:45.740 --> 00:58:47.960
thank you so much for that exposition.

00:58:47.960 --> 00:58:50.350
And I'd like to open up the floor now,

