if it would be possible

01:11:24.470 --> 01:11:27.690
to understand COVID as a black Swan,

01:11:27.690 --> 01:11:30.330
is it possible to cross data from natural

01:11:30.330 --> 01:11:32.170
and social sciences?

01:11:32.170 --> 01:11:33.540
Is it not a risk in

01:11:34.484 --> 01:11:38.013
I think constructing
artificial parallel trends?

01:11:42.821 --> 01:11:44.813
- Let me see if I can read
that one that says that from.

01:11:46.039 --> 01:11:48.840
- Further back, sometimes it's not easy

01:11:48.840 --> 01:11:50.950
to scroll through the chat feature,

01:11:50.950 --> 01:11:52.450
here, I'll just post it again.

01:12:03.370 --> 01:12:06.979
- Well, certainly, I mean,
certainly it's a black Swan

01:12:06.979 --> 01:12:08.770
in the sense that I
understand the black Swan

01:12:08.770 --> 01:12:09.850
is just something that nobody

01:12:09.850 --> 01:12:11.743
would have expected to have happened.

01:12:13.900 --> 01:12:17.023
So certainly it's a
black Swan in that sense.

01:12:19.292 --> 01:12:21.450
Is it possible to cross data
from natural social science?

01:12:21.450 --> 01:12:26.450
Yeah, look, I mean, I'm reasonably hopeful

01:12:28.070 --> 01:12:29.560
that in the long run as I said,

01:12:29.560 --> 01:12:31.930
we'll be able to sort
through some of this,

01:12:31.930 --> 01:12:35.540
we'll be able to disentangle
what some of the natural causes

01:12:35.540 --> 01:12:37.390
were from what, or just being,

01:12:37.390 --> 01:12:42.390
natural non-linear variability, et cetera,

01:12:43.420 --> 01:12:46.827
but not yet, I don't think so.

01:12:51.003 --> 01:12:53.150
- Okay, next question begins
with the favorable comment.

01:12:53.150 --> 01:12:55.440
Thanks for the fascinating talk.

01:12:55.440 --> 01:12:59.540
What evidential role do first
order causal/mechanistic

01:12:59.540 --> 01:13:03.520
explanations play in non-linear phenomena?

01:13:03.520 --> 01:13:05.320
For instance, let's say that prior

01:13:05.320 --> 01:13:08.150
to doing a large scale tourists program,

01:13:08.150 --> 01:13:10.450
there was a small focus group that showed

01:13:10.450 --> 01:13:14.400
that the intervention is
effective on a micro scale.

01:13:14.400 --> 01:13:17.070
Does this have any evidential value

01:13:17.070 --> 01:13:19.060
that the tourist intervention will

01:13:19.060 --> 01:13:23.460
have some efficacy when
put out on a large scale?

01:13:23.460 --> 01:13:25.030
- I mean that just case by case, right.

01:13:25.030 --> 01:13:27.060
I can't answer that generally.

01:13:27.060 --> 01:13:32.060
But I think often in the
case of like epidemics

01:13:33.036 --> 01:13:35.941
what's usually going on
with something like that

01:13:35.941 --> 01:13:39.973
is something that's being
tested in a kind of,

01:13:41.870 --> 01:13:43.951
micro clinical setting,

01:13:43.951 --> 01:13:45.320
and then being extrapolated
to a social setting.

01:13:45.320 --> 01:13:50.030
So, you might do something
like put masks on mannequins,

01:13:50.030 --> 01:13:52.760
in a room and have them
spray aerosols on it

01:13:52.760 --> 01:13:57.023
and see what portion of
the aerosol is trapping,

01:13:57.990 --> 01:14:01.180
but it doesn't tell you
whether you're preventing

01:14:02.434 --> 01:14:05.763
a minimum infectious dose from
getting to the other person.

01:14:09.032 --> 01:14:10.250
It certainly doesn't
tell you anything about

01:14:10.250 --> 01:14:13.413
what mask mandates do in
terms of mask wearing.

01:14:15.080 --> 01:14:18.310
You could be a hundred percent
convinced that mask wearing

01:14:18.310 --> 01:14:21.900
reduces spread without
believing that mask mandates do

01:14:21.900 --> 01:14:24.600
particularly maybe if you
thought that masks wearing

01:14:24.600 --> 01:14:28.516
for, so again, I think this is right.

01:14:28.516 --> 01:14:31.670
That's the sort of standard
view in clinical settings

01:14:31.670 --> 01:14:35.940
about N 95 masks was you
should have gone into a room

01:14:35.940 --> 01:14:37.940
with one of them hoping
that it will protect you

01:14:37.940 --> 01:14:39.820
for more than 20 minutes.

01:14:39.820 --> 01:14:41.190
That was I think the usual guideline,

01:14:41.190 --> 01:14:43.500
like go in there, wear your N 95 mask,

01:14:43.500 --> 01:14:45.390
and get out within 20 minutes.

01:14:45.390 --> 01:14:46.744
Why?

01:14:46.744 --> 01:14:48.490
Not because N 95 masks
only last 20 minutes

01:14:48.490 --> 01:14:49.680
or something like that.

01:14:49.680 --> 01:14:51.240
But because people are not very good

01:14:51.240 --> 01:14:53.964
at not sticking their fingers in there

01:14:53.964 --> 01:14:55.113
after about 20 minutes.

01:14:55.950 --> 01:14:57.900
What about people who were
working eight hour shifts

01:14:57.900 --> 01:14:58.733
with the mask?

01:14:59.934 --> 01:15:04.934
So yeah, I mean, obviously
you can get causal information

01:15:08.900 --> 01:15:10.320
from these abstract settings,

01:15:10.320 --> 01:15:13.210
like putting masks on mannequins

01:15:13.210 --> 01:15:15.620
and seeing what kind of
aerosols come through them

01:15:15.620 --> 01:15:16.610
or whatever,

01:15:16.610 --> 01:15:20.240
but getting from there to knowing what

01:15:20.240 --> 01:15:22.730
the actual impact of let's say,

01:15:22.730 --> 01:15:26.250
a mask mandate on an entire municipality

01:15:26.250 --> 01:15:28.389
or county or state or whatever,

01:15:28.389 --> 01:15:30.500
it's gotta be not just for one day,

01:15:30.500 --> 01:15:32.570
but for months, right?

01:15:32.570 --> 01:15:36.330
Again, remembering that
like, if this was a marathon,

01:15:36.330 --> 01:15:40.640
not a sprint, maybe something
that maybe protected people

01:15:40.640 --> 01:15:42.150
while they were complying perfectly

01:15:42.150 --> 01:15:45.210
for a few days might not have had value

01:15:45.210 --> 01:15:47.027
over the course of the months does,

01:15:47.027 --> 01:15:49.945
not need stopped enforced or whatever.

01:15:49.945 --> 01:15:53.554
So I think, there's a little bit of,

01:15:53.554 --> 01:15:56.150
you can glean from things like that,

01:15:56.150 --> 01:15:59.060
but I mean, there's a
reason why, for example,

01:16:01.810 --> 01:16:04.670
economists like to look at

01:16:04.670 --> 01:16:06.040
the difference in deference methods

01:16:06.040 --> 01:16:08.850
rather than just focus groups
or something like that,

01:16:08.850 --> 01:16:11.290
because it's difficult to extrapolate

01:16:11.290 --> 01:16:13.290
from those contexts to broader contexts.

01:16:15.668 --> 01:16:17.590
- But Eric, what do you
make of the six feet

01:16:17.590 --> 01:16:19.530
in the social distancing?

01:16:19.530 --> 01:16:20.680
Do you think that was--

01:16:21.820 --> 01:16:25.100
- I thought that was well
understood to have been made up

01:16:25.100 --> 01:16:29.877
and not particularly, my
understanding the latest

01:16:31.080 --> 01:16:33.142
while we spoke is that,

01:16:33.142 --> 01:16:37.910
it's more about a number
of people in the room,

01:16:37.910 --> 01:16:40.457
how much time they spend in the room,

01:16:40.457 --> 01:16:41.970
the quality of ventilation in the room,

01:16:41.970 --> 01:16:45.780
and not so much about, it's
not like in other words,

01:16:45.780 --> 01:16:48.690
I thought the consensus
now was not so much

01:16:48.690 --> 01:16:52.890
that I don't just directly
transmit the virus to you,

01:16:52.890 --> 01:16:54.743
I can fill the room up with it.

01:16:55.992 --> 01:16:59.190
And so the six feet, I think
most people started thinking

01:16:59.190 --> 01:17:00.173
it didn't matter that much.

01:17:02.760 --> 01:17:03.593
- Okay.

01:17:05.510 --> 01:17:07.960
And the last question I think
we have in the list here,

01:17:07.960 --> 01:17:11.610
what about things surfaces
versus airborne transmission

01:17:11.610 --> 01:17:13.370
building on what you were just saying

01:17:13.370 --> 01:17:15.920
that the latter is more important.

01:17:15.920 --> 01:17:19.450
Airborne transmission strikes
me as something most of us,

01:17:19.450 --> 01:17:21.740
myself included feel like we've learned

01:17:21.740 --> 01:17:23.573
over the course of the pandemic,

01:17:24.509 --> 01:17:26.910
and which has informed
reasonable changes of course,

01:17:26.910 --> 01:17:28.900
when it comes to mitigation strategies.

01:17:28.900 --> 01:17:31.510
So focusing less on
cleaning, for instance,

01:17:31.510 --> 01:17:34.450
more on ventilation, indoors and outdoors,

01:17:34.450 --> 01:17:37.010
the concrete version of
the question just asked,

01:17:37.010 --> 01:17:41.200
did we have no reason
for making those changes?

01:17:41.200 --> 01:17:43.590
- So, I mean, those are quite different,

01:17:43.590 --> 01:17:46.380
I mean, so I think at the surface thing,

01:17:46.380 --> 01:17:48.531
like we know like that's pretty clear,

01:17:48.531 --> 01:17:51.510
like floor mites I don't
transmit corona virus,

01:17:51.510 --> 01:17:53.860
it's just like the
washing of your groceries,

01:17:53.860 --> 01:17:55.603
all that was for nothing.

01:17:56.878 --> 01:18:01.878
I mean, that's like you can ruling out

01:18:02.060 --> 01:18:04.063
a potential causal mechanism,

01:18:05.505 --> 01:18:06.380
it's whole lot easier.

01:18:06.380 --> 01:18:07.470
I mean, it's not easy.

01:18:07.470 --> 01:18:09.000
It will still take us a few months,

01:18:09.000 --> 01:18:12.013
I take it or whatever, but that right.

01:18:12.013 --> 01:18:17.013
And separating again,
whether it's six feet

01:18:18.550 --> 01:18:20.530
versus filling with a room thing,

01:18:20.530 --> 01:18:23.740
I mean, those are things
that you can determine

01:18:23.740 --> 01:18:26.010
in a microscopic context.

01:18:26.010 --> 01:18:29.040
Like those are sort of
basic mechanical facts,

01:18:29.040 --> 01:18:31.730
even I think determined in
the microscopic context,

01:18:31.730 --> 01:18:33.925
but that's quite different from,

01:18:33.925 --> 01:18:37.680
you can make guesses from facts like that,

01:18:37.680 --> 01:18:40.720
to what interventions
are gonna do socially.

01:18:40.720 --> 01:18:43.683
But social interventions are
unbelievably complicated,

01:18:44.600 --> 01:18:49.226
I mean, again, this was
something that I think,

01:18:49.226 --> 01:18:51.230
it was widely known and understood

01:18:51.230 --> 01:18:55.470
among public health experts
prior to the COVID-19 pandemic,

01:18:55.470 --> 01:19:00.470
that closing schools for more
than six weeks is ineffective.

01:19:00.640 --> 01:19:01.932
Why?

01:19:01.932 --> 01:19:05.690
Not because of anything you could learn

01:19:05.690 --> 01:19:09.270
by watching what happens
with kids in school,

01:19:09.270 --> 01:19:10.890
and the way the virus is, no,

01:19:10.890 --> 01:19:15.780
because kids are kids, and
they crave social interaction.

01:19:15.780 --> 01:19:16.930
And if you close the schools,

01:19:16.930 --> 01:19:20.390
they eventually start
congregating in other ways.

01:19:20.390 --> 01:19:24.610
So, I like to remind people that the deem

01:19:24.610 --> 01:19:28.063
and epidemiology is from
the Greek word for people.

01:19:28.900 --> 01:19:32.460
And it's the things that
we're talking about here

01:19:32.460 --> 01:19:36.610
are not just microscopic facts about

01:19:36.610 --> 01:19:37.820
how the virus is transmitted,

01:19:37.820 --> 01:19:39.910
we're talking about like, what happens

01:19:39.910 --> 01:19:43.830
when you institute public health measures

01:19:43.830 --> 01:19:45.910
and you then have to wait and see like

01:19:45.910 --> 01:19:47.160
what people are gonna do.

01:19:48.980 --> 01:19:51.180
That's very different
than being able to predict

01:19:51.180 --> 01:19:54.173
what will happen if people
actually do exactly what you say,

01:19:57.020 --> 01:19:57.930
which never happens.

01:19:57.930 --> 01:20:00.630
People don't do exactly
what you say, never.

01:20:00.630 --> 01:20:05.630
That's not how policymaking works?

01:20:06.030 --> 01:20:08.870
When you're evaluating
a policy intervention,

01:20:08.870 --> 01:20:11.908
you don't just imagine that
everyone's gonna do exactly

01:20:11.908 --> 01:20:12.760
what you tell them to do,

01:20:12.760 --> 01:20:15.273
'cause they aren't built that way.

01:20:17.240 --> 01:20:22.240
- So I guess there's one
final question has come in.

01:20:23.140 --> 01:20:26.880
And the question is that it's been usual

01:20:26.880 --> 01:20:31.630
to miss cultural issues
when news presents data

01:20:31.630 --> 01:20:34.200
on how to think about cultural traditions

01:20:34.200 --> 01:20:36.603
that make for difficult comparisons.

01:20:37.720 --> 01:20:40.560
How does one take that into account?

01:20:40.560 --> 01:20:45.210
Do we need observations from
other feeds like ethnography?

01:20:45.210 --> 01:20:46.631
- Yeah.

01:20:46.631 --> 01:20:48.100
This was another thing
I think that set me off

01:20:48.100 --> 01:20:51.310
early on in the pandemic
about this kind of stuff.

01:20:51.310 --> 01:20:54.640
So I was in California early,

01:20:54.640 --> 01:20:56.560
and we started seeing
early on in California

01:20:56.560 --> 01:20:59.600
that like Latino communities were having

01:21:00.980 --> 01:21:04.560
more infection than
non-Latino communities.

01:21:04.560 --> 01:21:06.984
I started seeing people talk about, oh,

01:21:06.984 --> 01:21:09.680
it's because, fill in the blank about

01:21:09.680 --> 01:21:12.124
how the Latino people behave.

01:21:12.124 --> 01:21:13.277
And I just thought (indistinct),

01:21:16.530 --> 01:21:20.010
my wild guess isn't because
these people have jobs

01:21:20.010 --> 01:21:22.023
that don't allow them to work over zoom,

01:21:23.857 --> 01:21:27.260
let's not leap to like stereotypes about

01:21:27.260 --> 01:21:30.623
how various ethnic groups
like comport themselves.

01:21:31.720 --> 01:21:35.700
And so that was one thing
that I think set me off

01:21:35.700 --> 01:21:39.010
kind of early about, like,
let's not be super hasty

01:21:39.010 --> 01:21:41.340
in making these causal attributions.

01:21:42.180 --> 01:21:44.610
- But on the other hand,
I mean it is undeniable

01:21:44.610 --> 01:21:48.450
that communities of color
were hit much, much harder,

01:21:48.450 --> 01:21:51.867
both economically, epidemiologically,

01:21:51.867 --> 01:21:54.260
and in terms of, we already knew

01:21:54.260 --> 01:21:59.260
that the medical care
for these communities

01:21:59.610 --> 01:22:01.930
is not as robust as it is.

01:22:01.930 --> 01:22:06.220
- Absolutely, I mean, this
plus I never seen this

01:22:09.557 --> 01:22:11.320
I think maybe how to do
that asked a question,

01:22:11.320 --> 01:22:12.900
and then they pointed this out to me,

01:22:12.900 --> 01:22:17.900
somebody did early on that the number

01:22:18.472 --> 01:22:21.310
of hospital beds per capita in Manhattan

01:22:21.310 --> 01:22:24.540
was like three or four
times higher than in Queens.

01:22:24.540 --> 01:22:26.840
And lo and behold, they were bad.

01:22:26.840 --> 01:22:31.260
Were the hospitals in New York
overwhelmed than in Queens?

01:22:31.260 --> 01:22:34.070
And of course also ran,
I mean, that policy,

01:22:34.070 --> 01:22:37.700
look, I mean, these policy interventions

01:22:37.700 --> 01:22:42.700
were being made by wealthy white people.

01:22:44.670 --> 01:22:49.670
And so they were, a lot of them
were designed to protect us.

01:22:51.330 --> 01:22:53.860
We were the ones deciding
what were essential services

01:22:53.860 --> 01:22:56.760
and what weren't essential
services, et cetera, et cetera.

01:22:58.470 --> 01:23:01.440
And then of course also in US particularly

01:23:01.440 --> 01:23:04.549
among African-Americans obviously,

01:23:04.549 --> 01:23:08.000
we unfortunately have kind
of preexisting bad health.

01:23:12.460 --> 01:23:15.350
That's obviously the results of various

01:23:15.350 --> 01:23:17.480
other racial qualities, et cetera.

01:23:17.480 --> 01:23:20.150
So there's a whole
complex mixture in there,

01:23:20.150 --> 01:23:25.150
but I really doubt, I mean,
I shouldn't say I doubt,

01:23:25.900 --> 01:23:28.403
I should just say you'll
be on my back harder,

01:23:29.651 --> 01:23:31.210
to think that what's going on there

01:23:31.210 --> 01:23:33.715
is something like, oh,

01:23:33.715 --> 01:23:37.540
it's because Latino
people behave this way,

01:23:37.540 --> 01:23:40.500
or it's because black people
behave that way or whatever.

01:23:40.500 --> 01:23:42.323
Like, let's leave that for last.

01:23:43.420 --> 01:23:44.890
Yeah.

01:23:44.890 --> 01:23:48.031
- And curiously, that seems
to be where people go first.

01:23:48.031 --> 01:23:48.864
- [Eric] Yeah, of course, of course.

01:23:48.864 --> 01:23:50.283
- Absolutely, absolutely.

